{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba .github/workflows/deploy-railway.yml'","aggregated_output":"     1\tname: deploy-railway\n     2\t# Automated redeploy of the apps/site Next app (marketing + docs + /dashboard), the apps/demos\n     3\t# service it rewrites /demos/* to (ADR-0400), apps/admin, and — since ADR-0414 — the two services\n     4\t# that had NO automated deploy path at all: services/docs (the RAG API) and services/support-bot\n     5\t# (the Discord bot). Plus services/license on dispatch. This is the whole Railway fleet.\n     6\t# Build-on-Railway via the service's RAILWAY_DOCKERFILE_PATH=apps/site/Dockerfile env var (set on the\n     7\t# Railway service, not here). Touches NO DNS and NO CF Access.\n     8\t#\n     9\t# ARMED since 2026-07-29: RAILWAY_TOKEN (a project token scoped to caisson-prod) is a repo secret and\n    10\t# this workflow deploys for real. The arm guard below stays, because it is what keeps the workflow\n    11\t# honest if the secret is ever rotated out or revoked: the guard step reads the token and SKIPS every\n    12\t# real step when it is absent, so a push-triggered run degrades to a GREEN no-op rather than reddening\n    13\t# main. ONE EXCEPTION: a caller that passes `require_armed=true` (the release train's leg 4) gets a hard\n    14\t# FAILURE instead of the green skip, because a train that reports a deploy it never performed is worse\n    15\t# than a red leg.\n    16\t# The static-export deploy-site.yml (Cloudflare Pages) it once coexisted with is already retired (its\n    17\t# CI half); deleting the Pages project itself is the DEPLOY-class teardown step C7.\n    18\ton:\n    19\t  push:\n    20\t    branches: [main]\n    21\t    # These paths gate the AUTOMATIC path only, which deploys admin + demos + site + docs +\n    22\t    # support-bot (everything except license, which is dispatch-only — see its step).\n    23\t    # `services/license/**` is deliberately absent: the license step is dispatch-only (it runs\n    24\t    # migrations — see the step). `apps/admin/**` is present because this job deploys admin and an\n    25\t    # admin-only change previously did not trigger it at all, so admin could drift behind main\n    26\t    # until some other tree changed. `apps/demos/**` is present for the same reason (ADR-0400):\n    27\t    # the demo surface is its own deploy unit now, and a demos-only change must reach it.\n    28\t    # Keep every deployed app and its non-package runtime workspaces in this filter: site and\n    29\t    # admin compile the demo registry, which in turn imports the audit harness.\n    30\t    paths:\n    31\t      - \"apps/site/**\"\n    32\t      - \"apps/admin/**\"\n    33\t      - \"apps/demos/**\"\n    34\t      # ADR-0414: both had NO automated deploy path — no workflow referenced either service, so\n    35\t      # the only way either shipped was an operator hand-run of tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts.\n    36\t      # docs/deploy/STATE.md and docs/state/production-readiness.md both claimed they \"deploy only\n    37\t      # via the release train\", which was never true: leg 4 dispatches THIS workflow, and this\n    38\t      # workflow did not know they existed.\n    39\t      - \"services/docs/**\"\n    40\t      - \"services/support-bot/**\"\n    41\t      - \"packages/**\"\n    42\t      - \"tooling/audit-harness/**\"\n    43\t      - \"tooling/demo-registry/**\"\n    44\t      - \".github/workflows/deploy-railway.yml\"\n    45\t      # Root dep manifests: a root-only dependency bump (override/lockfile) changes every\n    46\t      # service's built bytes but matched nothing above — the 2026-08-10 dep wave (#425,\n    47\t      # dompurify XSS fix in the site bundle) shipped with no auto-deploy and needed a\n    48\t      # manual dispatch.\n    49\t      - \"package.json\"\n    50\t      - \"bun.lock\"\n    51\t  workflow_dispatch:\n    52\t    inputs:\n    53\t      ref:\n    54\t        description: \"The git ref to deploy (checked out, ancestry-verified, archived clean)\"\n    55\t        required: false\n    56\t        default: \"\"\n    57\t      require_armed:\n    58\t        description: \"Set 'true' to FAIL when RAILWAY_TOKEN is absent instead of skipping green (the release train passes this)\"\n    59\t        required: false\n    60\t        default: \"false\"\n    61\t\n    62\tpermissions:\n    63\t  contents: read\n    64\t\n    65\tconcurrency:\n    66\t  group: deploy-railway\n    67\t  cancel-in-progress: false\n    68\t\n    69\tjobs:\n    70\t  deploy:\n    71\t    # Blacksmith-only fleet (operator directive 2026-07-19, ADR-0365 lock-1 provision): the last\n    72\t    # hosted job followed the rest after the free-minute exhaustion made it a permanent zero-step\n    73\t    # red. Operator lock ADR-0367: this job STAYS on Blacksmith even once RAILWAY_TOKEN arms —\n    74\t    # VM-per-job isolation + a Railway-scoped revocable deploy credential is the accepted posture.\n    75\t    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n    76\t    # This cap MUST exceed the sum of the steps' own poll budgets, or it silently reintroduces at\n    77\t    # the JOB level the exact defect it was raised to fix at the STEP level. The budgets are 15 min\n    78\t    # each (railway-deploy.ts's default) except docs at 40:\n    79\t    #   push path      admin 15 + demos 15 + site 15 + docs 40 + bot 15  = 100 min\n    80\t    #   dispatch path  + license 15                                      = 115 min\n    81\t    # plus setup (a fetch-depth:0 checkout of a 72-workspace monorepo, setup-bun, the railway CLI\n    82\t    # install) ≈ 2-5 min. 125 covers the dispatch ceiling with headroom.\n    83\t    #\n    84\t    # 25 was the original value, already EXACTLY equal to caisson-docs' railway.toml\n    85\t    # healthcheckTimeout (1500s). A first pass raised it to 70 reasoning \"five fast services plus\n    86\t    # one docs boot\" — self-refuting arithmetic: five services at the 15-minute default is 75\n    87\t    # before docs' 40 is counted at all. A job-level cancel is strictly worse than a step timeout:\n    88\t    # no \"timed out waiting for the X deployment\" diagnostic is printed (that only fires when a\n    89\t    # STEP budget expires), and the likeliest cancellation point is the LAST step — leaving a new\n    90\t    # docs API serving an old bot, the exact skew the docs -> support-bot order exists to prevent.\n    91\t    timeout-minutes: 125\n    92\t    steps:\n    93\t      # Inert-until-armed guard: `secrets` can't be referenced in a job/step `if:`, so map RAILWAY_TOKEN\n    94\t      # to an env here and publish an `armed` output. Absent token → armed=false → every step below skips\n    95\t      # → the job succeeds as a no-op. This is what keeps main GREEN before the operator provisions the\n    96\t      # token; it does NOT arm the deploy (no token is added here — it self-arms when the secret is set).\n    97\t      - name: arm check (RAILWAY_TOKEN present?)\n    98\t        id: guard\n    99\t        env:\n   100\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   101\t          REQUIRE_ARMED: ${{ github.event.inputs.require_armed || 'false' }}\n   102\t        run: |\n   103\t          if [ -n \"${RAILWAY_TOKEN}\" ]; then\n   104\t            echo \"armed=true\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   105\t          elif [ \"${REQUIRE_ARMED}\" = \"true\" ]; then\n   106\t            # Called by an ARMED release train. A green skip here is a false green of the worst\n   107\t            # kind: the train reports every leg passed while the site still serves the previous\n   108\t            # image, and the only thing that catches it is someone reading the live page. Leg 1\n   109\t            # (registry publish) already refuses to no-op with missing credentials for exactly\n   110\t            # this reason; this is that rule applied to the deploy leg.\n   111\t            echo \"::error::deploy-railway was called with require_armed=true but RAILWAY_TOKEN is not set — refusing to report a green deploy that shipped nothing. Arm the secret, or deploy from the operator box with tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts.\"\n   112\t            exit 1\n   113\t          else\n   114\t            echo \"armed=false\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   115\t            echo \"::notice::deploy-railway is inert — RAILWAY_TOKEN not set; skipping deploy (operator arms at cutover C2)\"\n   116\t          fi\n   117\t      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7\n   118\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   119\t        with:\n   120\t          # Full history: tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts resolves --ref and checks its ancestry\n   121\t          # against origin/main itself, so the checkout needs every commit + branch it might touch.\n   122\t          fetch-depth: 0\n   123\t      # A manual dispatch can leave `ref` blank; default to the SHA that triggered this run (the\n   124\t      # push SHA, or the workflow_dispatch ref's tip) — mirrors publish.yml's tag-defaulting.\n   125\t      - name: resolve deploy ref\n   126\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   127\t        id: resolve-ref\n   128\t        env:\n   129\t          RAW_REF: ${{ github.event.inputs.ref }}\n   130\t        run: |\n   131\t          echo \"ref=${RAW_REF:-${{ github.sha }}}\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   132\t      - name: fetch origin/main (ancestry check needs it)\n   133\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   134\t        run: git fetch origin main --quiet\n   135\t      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n   136\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   137\t        with:\n   138\t          bun-version: \"1.4.2\"\n   139\t      - name: install Railway CLI\n   140\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   141\t        run: bun add -g @railway/cli@5.23.3\n   142\t      # Immutable-input deploy: resolves the ref to a commit, refuses one that isn't an ancestor\n   143\t      # of origin/main, stages a CLEAN `git archive` tree (no dirty working-tree state, no\n   144\t      # untracked cruft, git's own normalized file modes), runs `railway up` from that staging\n   145\t      # dir, and appends a single-use receipt — never a bare `railway up` off the live checkout.\n   146\t      #\n   147\t      # ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: admin -> license -> site. Two independent constraints pin it:\n   148\t      #\n   149\t      # 1. Verifier before issuer, on BOTH credential layers. admin verifies the internal-proof\n   150\t      #    bearer that site issues, and admin + the registry Worker (leg 1b, already live) verify\n   151\t      #    the license TOKENS that services/license mints. The verifier must already understand\n   152\t      #    every format the live issuer can send, which only verifier-first guarantees: a new\n   153\t      #    issuer against an old verifier is a hard 401 on every buyer's evidence dashboard (the\n   154\t      #    pre-F3 verifier regex `^Bearer ([0-9a-f]{64})$` could not match a\n   155\t      #    `<unix-seconds>.<hmac>` credential at all). Found by the v2026.07.30 release audit:\n   156\t      #    until then this job deployed ONLY caisson-site, so no admin change had ever ridden the\n   157\t      #    train and admin silently drifted behind main. The dual-format acceptance that made the\n   158\t      #    F3 rollout itself safe is GONE as of 2026-07-31 (see internal-proof-auth.ts) — that was\n   159\t      #    a one-release transition, not the rule. The rule is this ordering.\n   160\t      #\n   161\t      # 2. Schema before the image that reads it. license's `preDeployCommand`\n   162\t      #    (`bun apps/site/lib/deploy-migrate.ts` — see services/license/railway.toml) is the ONLY\n   163\t      #    path that applies platform migrations to production; site's standalone image cannot run\n   164\t      #    the orchestrator. Deploying site first would put a new image live against a schema its\n   165\t      #    release's migrations have not reached — the CAISSON-64 class (`byok_key_meta` was never\n   166\t      #    created in prod and /dashboard/ai-keys crashed for every account). So license, the\n   167\t      #    migration carrier, deploys before site. railway.toml states the same rule.\n   168\t      #\n   169\t      # admin -> license -> demos -> site satisfies both: admin (verifier of both credentials)\n   170\t      # leads, license follows its verifiers and applies the schema, demos lands before the image\n   171\t      # whose rewrite points at it (ADR-0400), and site lands last on the schema it needs. The\n   172\t      # launch runbook's \"license issuer last\" was only safe because its step 5 applies the\n   173\t      # migration chain BY HAND before step 6 — this workflow has no hand-applied step 5, so the\n   174\t      # migration carrier cannot go last here.\n   175\t      #\n   176\t      # 3. ADR-0414 appends docs -> support-bot AFTER site, in that order, on the same\n   177\t      #    dependency-before-dependent rule that puts demos before site: the bot READS the docs\n   178\t      #    service (DOCS_SERVICE_URL=https://docs-api.caisson.sh, DOCS_SERVICE_TOKEN shared —\n   179\t      #    services/support-bot/railway.toml), so the API it queries must already be serving this\n   180\t      #    release before the client that queries it goes live. They go after site rather than\n   181\t      #    before it because nothing in the site image points at either, and because docs is the\n   182\t      #    slowest leg by an order of magnitude — putting a 20-minute cold-boot re-embed ahead of\n   183\t      #    the buyer-facing site deploy would delay every release's most visible surface behind\n   184\t      #    the least visible one.\n   185\t      - name: deploy apps/admin to Railway (verifier — MUST precede both issuers)\n   186\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   187\t        env:\n   188\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   189\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   190\t        run: |\n   191\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   192\t            --service caisson-admin \\\n   193\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\"\n   194\t      # Including license AT ALL is the fix for a class the parity probe caught 2026-07-31: license\n   195\t      # bakes registry/index.json into its image exactly as the Worker bundle and the admin image\n   196\t      # do, but leg 4 deployed only admin and site. So every release moved repo, Worker and admin\n   197\t      # onto the new index and left license serving the previous one (`4810e38157c1` vs\n   198\t      # `dc5ee000aebf`, one full release behind, closed by a hand redeploy).\n   199\t      #\n   200\t      # DISPATCH-ONLY, deliberately. license is the ONLY one of the three carrying a\n   201\t      # preDeployCommand, so putting it on the automatic push-triggered path would run schema\n   202\t      # migrations against production on any merge touching packages/**, unattended. A\n   203\t      # workflow_dispatch is an explicit act: the release train's leg 4, or an operator by hand.\n   204\t      # Push-triggered runs keep deploying admin and site only, exactly as before — which also\n   205\t      # means a push-triggered site deploy carrying a new migration still needs the operator (or\n   206\t      # the train) to run the license leg; that gap is the price of never migrating unattended.\n   207\t      - name: deploy services/license to Railway (token issuer + migration carrier; dispatch only)\n   208\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true' && github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'\n   209\t        env:\n   210\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   211\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   212\t        run: |\n   213\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   214\t            --service caisson-license \\\n   215\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\"\n   216\t      # apps/demos (ADR-0400) — the demo surface apps/site rewrites /demos/* to. It deploys BEFORE\n   217\t      # site for the same reason license does: site's image points at it, so the target must\n   218\t      # already be serving the release the pointer is shipped with. Deploying site first would put\n   219\t      # a new set of embed URLs live against the previous demos image.\n   220\t      #\n   221\t      # SECOND GUARD, ON PURPOSE. Creating the Railway service was an external-system act the\n   222\t      # operator performed (ADR-0400 decision 4), and this workflow merged before that happened —\n   223\t      # `railway up --service caisson-demos` against a service that does not exist is a hard\n   224\t      # failure, which on an ARMED token would redden this job and skip the site deploy below it.\n   225\t      # A missing entry would have been the safer failure, so the entry gates itself: the repo\n   226\t      # variable CAISSON_DEMOS_ARMED must read exactly \"true\". `vars` (unlike `secrets`) is\n   227\t      # readable in an `if:`, so this needs no guard step of its own. That variable has read \"true\"\n   228\t      # since 2026-08-09, so the step now runs on every armed deploy; the gate stays because\n   229\t      # clearing the variable is the documented way to take the demos surface out of the fleet\n   230\t      # without editing this file.\n   231\t      - name: deploy apps/demos to Railway (rewrite target — MUST precede site)\n   232\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true' && vars.CAISSON_DEMOS_ARMED == 'true'\n   233\t        env:\n   234\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   235\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   236\t        run: |\n   237\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   238\t            --service caisson-demos \\\n   239\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\"\n   240\t      - name: deploy apps/site to Railway (immutable-input, receipt-gated)\n   241\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   242\t        env:\n   243\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   244\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   245\t        run: |\n   246\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   247\t            --service caisson-site \\\n   248\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\"\n   249\t      # services/docs — the RAG API behind docs-api.caisson.sh (ADR-0096/0105). ONE guard only,\n   250\t      # the same `armed` check every other step uses: the service has existed and been Online\n   251\t      # since 2026-06-30, so there is no missing-service hazard of the kind that earned demos its\n   252\t      # second CAISSON_DEMOS_ARMED gate.\n   253\t      #\n   254\t      # --wait-minutes 40 is load-bearing, not padding. This service answers /health with a 503\n   255\t      # {\"warming\":true} while it embeds its corpus, its railway.toml sets healthcheckTimeout=1500\n   256\t      # (25 min), and DOCS_EMBED_PHASE_DEADLINE_MS is 20 min in the service env because the volume\n   257\t      # embed-cache does not persist across boots — so a cold boot legitimately re-embeds from\n   258\t      # scratch. railway-deploy.ts's default poll budget is 15 minutes; at the default this step\n   259\t      # would throw \"timed out waiting for the deployment to reach a terminal state\" on a deploy\n   260\t      # that was still succeeding, and take the support-bot step down with it.\n   261\t      - name: deploy services/docs to Railway (RAG API — slow cold boot, see --wait-minutes)\n   262\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   263\t        env:\n   264\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   265\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   266\t        run: |\n   267\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   268\t            --service caisson-docs \\\n   269\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\" \\\n   270\t            --wait-minutes 40\n   271\t      # services/support-bot — the Discord bot (ADR-0009/0105), the repo's only Python service.\n   272\t      # LAST, and after docs on purpose: it is a CLIENT of the docs service above, so the API must\n   273\t      # already serve this release before the bot that calls it restarts against it.\n   274\t      - name: deploy services/support-bot to Railway (docs client — MUST follow docs)\n   275\t        if: steps.guard.outputs.armed == 'true'\n   276\t        env:\n   277\t          RAILWAY_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RAILWAY_TOKEN }}\n   278\t          DEPLOY_REF: ${{ steps.resolve-ref.outputs.ref }}\n   279\t        run: |\n   280\t          bun tooling/scripts/railway-deploy.ts \\\n   281\t            --service caisson-support-bot \\\n   282\t            --ref \"$DEPLOY_REF\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
