{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba apps/site/lib/analytics-host.ts'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// Runtime-origin gate for the two client analytics senders (components/web-vitals-send.ts and\n     2\t// components/posthog-init.tsx). Both read `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY`, and both used to treat the\n     3\t// mere presence of that key as permission to report into caisson-prod. That is the wrong gate.\n     4\t//\n     5\t// `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are INLINED into the client bundle by Next at build time (ADR-0224), and\n     6\t// the key is a public `phc_` ingest token rather than a secret — it is *meant* to ship in client\n     7\t// JS. The consequence is that any artifact built on a machine where that env is exported carries\n     8\t// caisson-prod's key: a `bun run dev` in this repo, a `next build` of an unrelated project on the\n     9\t// same box, a preview deploy. Every one of those then beacons real events into the production\n    10\t// project, indistinguishable from traffic.\n    11\t//\n    12\t// The runtime origin is the only honest discriminator, so it is the gate. This makes inlining\n    13\t// harmless by construction: a bundle carrying the key but served from anywhere other than the\n    14\t// production hosts simply never sends, no matter which repo built it.\n    15\timport { SITE_URL } from \"./metadata\";\n    16\t\n    17\tconst APEX = new URL(SITE_URL).host;\n    18\t\n    19\t/**\n    20\t * The hosts this site is actually served from in production.\n    21\t *\n    22\t * Both the apex and `www` answer 200 directly — Cloudflare proxies both CNAMEs to the same Railway\n    23\t * service and there is no www→apex redirect (no middleware, no `redirects()` entry), so omitting\n    24\t * `www` here would silently blind real traffic rather than only blocking noise.\n    25\t */\n    26\texport const PRODUCTION_ANALYTICS_HOSTS: readonly string[] = [\n    27\t  APEX,\n    28\t  `www.${APEX}`,\n    29\t];\n    30\t\n    31\t/** True only for a host the production site actually serves from. Port-bearing hosts (`:3000`) and\n    32\t * every preview/localhost origin fall through to false. */\n    33\texport function isProductionAnalyticsHost(host: string | undefined): boolean {\n    34\t  return host !== undefined && PRODUCTION_ANALYTICS_HOSTS.includes(host);\n    35\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
