module-SKU wiring 2026-07-02). MUST match the same slug's row there EXACTLY — the cart\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:51: *  the entitlement (ADR-0071/0113); a mismatch fails resolvePurchase closed and a module purchase\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:114:/** The six bundles as cart-ready catalog items, each carrying its real Paddle price id. */\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:129:/** Every à-la-carte module as a cart-ready catalog item. Post-W7 every sellable SKU is\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:145: *  `lib/cart.ts` `pruneCart` (a persisted cart line carrying a retired id — the four ADR-0238\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:152:/** The cart-ready catalog item for a bundle slug (`compliance`, `everything`, …). */\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:157:/** The cart-ready catalog item for a module slug (`field-crypto`, `ai-meter`, …). */\napps/site/lib/catalog.ts:162:/** Project a catalog item down to the `CartItem` shape the cart persists (drops `blurb`). */\napps/site/lib/analytics.ts:7:  | \"add_to_cart\"\napps/site/lib/analytics.ts:8:  | \"view_cart\"\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:1:// The G13 \"owned\" derivation (ADR-0293) — pure so it is unit-testable without a DB. `/dashboard/plan`\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:2:// used to hardcode `owned=false` for every subscription row (a subscribed buyer saw a live Buy button\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:3:// on their own plan). The fix: an entitlement-bearing plan (e.g. Compliance Updates) is owned when\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:4:// EVERY entitlement it grants is active for the account — the SAME check the Purchases section\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:5:// already used correctly. A ZERO-entitlement plan (Developer, ADR-0269 Decision 3 — it never grants a\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:7:// \"owned\" for every account, subscribed or not. For that shape, ownership comes from the\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:10:  entitlements: readonly string[],\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:15:  return entitlements.length > 0\napps/site/lib/plan-owned.ts:16:    ? entitlements.every((id) => activeEntitlementIds.has(id))\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:12:import { cartItemSchema } from \"./cart\";\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:67:  test(\"every sellable module has a cart catalog item (W7 wired the full catalog)\", () => {\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:77:  test(\"every module price id resolves in the pricebook PURCHASE_BOOK to its own entitlement (cart→webhook grant path)\", () => {\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:78:    // The cart passes catalog.priceId to Paddle.Checkout; the webhook resolves that SAME id in the\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:79:    // pricebook to grant the entitlement. A key-convention mismatch (the bug this guards) fails\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:82:    // grant exactly the entitlement the buyer paid for.\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:87:      expect(entry?.entitlements).toEqual([slug]);\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:92:  test(\"every bundle price id resolves in the pricebook PURCHASE_BOOK to its canonical bundle entitlement\", () => {\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:93:    // Same cart→webhook invariant for the six bundles: each W7 bundle price id must grant exactly\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:99:      expect(entry?.entitlements).toEqual([slug]);\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:106:  test(\"projects a catalog item to a schema-valid cart item (drops blurb)\", () => {\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:110:    const cartItem = toCartItem(source);\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:111:    expect(cartItemSchema.safeParse(cartItem).success).toBe(true);\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:112:    expect(\"blurb\" in cartItem).toBe(false);\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:117:  test(\"exactly one non-PLACEHOLDER PURCHASE_BOOK row is tagged credit_pack, granting credits + no entitlements\", () => {\napps/site/lib/catalog.test.ts:127:    expect(entry.entitlements).toEqual([]);\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:5:// float, ADR-0007). Single source — pages, SKU grid, cart, and JSON-LD Offers all read from here\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:12:// bundle's saving vs the live à-la-carte catalog subtotal (`everythingSavings()`) — a real saving,\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:111:    note: \"The full catalog: every bundle and every à-la-carte module, one purchase.\",\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:115:/** Every module sold à la carte. Prices are the locked $49-$299 band (pricebook `SKU_RETAIL`). */\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:117:  /** Slug — the cart key and the Plausible event prop. */\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:399:/** À-la-carte subtotal of every sellable SKU (the real \"buy each module separately\" baseline). */\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:404:/** À-la-carte subtotal of a persona bundle's priced member modules — the real \"buy the members\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:411:/** What the Everything bundle saves vs buying every à-la-carte module separately, in whole USD —\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:420:/** Updates-renewal rate: a 12-month per-entitlement renewal costs a flat 40% of the\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:426:/** Renewal display price for a purchased entitlement id (module or bundle), whole USD.\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:429:export function renewalAmount(entitlementId: string): number | null {\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:431:    MODULE_PRICES.find((m) => m.id === entitlementId)?.amount ??\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:432:    BUNDLE_PRICES.find((b) => b.id === entitlementId)?.amount ??\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:471: *  number never drifts and — while `null` — never invents one. Operator-owned: `null` until the\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:473: *  are operator-owned\"). A plain business-days/hours STRING, not a numeric field, so it can carry\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:500:    note: \"Take a single module à la carte: 27 standalone modules across the catalog.\",\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:520:    // fail-closed GATE, not a display choice: unlike the six bundles / à-la-carte modules, this id is\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:521:    // never wired into `catalog.ts`'s `BUNDLE_PRICE_IDS`/`MODULE_PRICE_IDS` cart maps, and it carries\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:523:    // been created, per the picker lock: no Paddle API calls this track) — so there is no cart entry,\napps/site/lib/pricing.ts:554:/** Render a bare integer USD amount, e.g. 299 -> \"$299\" — for module/cart/savings figures that\napps/site/lib/site-migrations.ts:94:  // prod by the compliance-core entitlement gate short-circuiting before any table read.\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:13://    inbox ownership, so the trust boundary needs the extra round trip. Both the verification and\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:52: * session material (just `\"1\"`) and exists purely so `GET /api/cart/owned`\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:53: * (`app/api/cart/owned/route.ts`) can short-circuit BEFORE resolving a session or touching the DB,\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:55: * client-side instead: the owned-items provider (`components/owned-items-provider.tsx`) read this\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:97:    // this Set-Cookie just costs one wasted `/api/cart/owned` fetch (the provider's own fail-open\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:98:    // contract), never a false \"owned\" marking.\napps/site/lib/auth-server.ts:197:      // A password alone doesn't prove inbox ownership the way a clicked magic link does —\napps/site/lib/jsonld.ts:29: * Caisson Software LLC and GridWork Digital LLC are SEPARATE Georgia LLCs with common ownership\napps/site/lib/jsonld.ts:150: * ItemList of the à-la-carte modules for the marketplace Modules tab (ADR-0237 F1). Each element\napps/site/lib/public-onboarding-pages.test.tsx:22:    expect(html).toContain(\"revokes the license entitlement\");\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:6:  // The checkout reads the pre-declared pricebook credit map; it never recomputes bundle − owned ad-hoc.\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:13:  test(\"crediting matrix across owned sets (Compliance $1,649)\", () => {\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:14:    // [owned bare slugs] → expected credit off the $1,649 Compliance bundle.\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:19:      // an owned NON-member (ai-meter) is ignored, not credited\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:22:    for (const [owned, credit] of cases) {\napps/site/lib/upgrade-quote.test.ts:23:      const q = bundleUpgradeQuote(\"compliance\", owned);\napps/site/lib/subprocessors.ts:32:      \"Purchase and entitlement records\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:286:      ctaLabel: \"Add to cart\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1329:      \"Offline license verification is checking a signed license token's authenticity and entitlements with no network call, against a public key baked into the software itself. Caisson's license-verify package verifies the Ed25519 signature over canonicalized claims, then trusts only the signed tier; any failure, from a missing token to an elapsed expiry, resolves safely to the free community tier.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1334:      code: 'export function verifyLicenseWithKey(\\n  token: string | null | undefined,\\n  publicKey: KeyObject,\\n  now: Date = new Date(),\\n): VerifiedLicense {\\n  if (token === null || token === undefined || token === \"\") {\\n    return COMMUNITY;\\n  }\\n  try {\\n    const decoded = decodeToken(token);\\n    const signedBytes = Buffer.from(decoded.payload, \"utf8\");\\n\\n    // Asymmetric verify over the EXACT signed bytes (Ed25519: algorithm = null). `crypto.verify`,\\n    // not `timingSafeEqual`: a signature check is not a secret comparison (ADR-0010).\\n    if (!cryptoVerify(null, signedBytes, publicKey, decoded.signature)) {\\n      return COMMUNITY;\\n    }\\n\\n    const parsed = licenseClaimsSchema.safeParse(\\n      JSON.parse(decoded.payload) as unknown,\\n    );\\n    if (!parsed.success) {\\n      return COMMUNITY;\\n    }\\n    const claims = parsed.data;\\n\\n    // ... format-conformance (canonicalize(claims) === decoded.payload) and expiry checks follow,\\n    // each failing safe to COMMUNITY too ...\\n\\n    return {\\n      valid: true,\\n      tier: claims.tier,\\n      entitlements: claims.entitlements,\\n      eval: claims.eval === true, // the eval-license discriminator (watermarking, no-redistribution)\\n      claims,\\n    };\\n  } catch {\\n    // Any unexpected throw (JSON parse, codec edge, crypto) → community. The verifier never raises.\\n    return COMMUNITY;\\n  }\\n}',\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1359:          \"The verifier ships with one Ed25519 public key baked into the package at build time. It checks a token's signature against that key locally with node:crypto's verify(), then reads the tier and entitlements straight out of the signed claims. There is no license-server round trip and no network egress at verify time.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1365:          \"Every one of those cases returns the same result: the free community tier, zero entitlements, valid set to false. verifyLicenseWithKey never throws, so a corrupted, forged, or absent token degrades an install to the free tier instead of crashing it.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1377:          \"Not by itself. A signed token that verifies once verifies until its expiry, or forever for a perpetual major, and an air-gapped check has no revocation list to consult. Caisson enforces revocation on the network-connected surfaces instead, the license-issuer and entitlement-resolver paths, for accounts that need a license pulled.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1387:      \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1442:          \"No. POST /issue resolves entitlements from the account's own row under RLS before signing, so a caller can only receive what it already purchased, and no one can forge the signature itself without the private key that never leaves the issuer.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1451:      \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1456:    slug: \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1457:    term: \"Software entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1460:      \"A software entitlement is the record of exactly which purchased ids (bundles, individual modules) an account is currently allowed to use. Caisson resolves entitlements from a signed license token at the registry edge, cross-checked against a live revocation deny-set, so a lapsed or revoked purchase reverts to the free base view immediately.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1463:        \"makeLicenseEntitlementResolver: verify the license token, check the edge revocation deny-set, return the entitlement ids or null\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1465:      code: 'export function makeLicenseEntitlementResolver(\\n  getDenied: () => ReadonlySet<string>,\\n  verify: (token: string) => VerifiedLicense = verifyLicense,\\n): (request: Request) => ResolvedLicense | null {\\n  return (request: Request): ResolvedLicense | null => {\\n    const header = request.headers.get(\"authorization\");\\n    if (header === null) return null;\\n    const match = BEARER_RE.exec(header.trim());\\n    const token = match?.[1];\\n    if (token === undefined) return null;\\n    const verified = verify(token);\\n    if (!verified.valid || verified.claims === null) return null;\\n    // Edge revocation gate: an operator-revoked license id resolves to community, base-only.\\n    if (getDenied().has(verified.claims.licenseId)) return null;\\n    // The signed per-entitlement updates windows + entitledSince snapshots ride along; an\\n    // absent/null claim normalizes to the empty map (every entitlement unbounded / grandfathered).\\n    return {\\n      entitlements: verified.entitlements,\\n      updatesWindows: verified.claims.updatesWindows ?? {},\\n      entitledSince: verified.claims.entitledSince ?? {},\\n    };\\n  };\\n}',\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1470:        body: \"The resolver never trusts the token's cosmetic TIER string. verifyLicense checks the cryptographically signed claims.entitlements field alone, so editing the wire tier without a matching signature grants nothing.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1478:        body: \"The entitlement_grant junction stores one row per account, entitlement, and source. An account holds an entitlement while at least one active grant backs it, so a subscription and a one-time purchase of the same bundle each have to be revoked before access is lost.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1487:        question: \"What is a software entitlement?\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1489:          \"A software entitlement is the specific set of purchased ids, bundles, or individual modules, an account is currently allowed to use. Caisson resolves it at request time from a signed license token, not from a client-supplied claim, so the caller can never assert its own access.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1493:          \"How does Caisson check entitlements at the edge without a database round trip?\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1501:          \"Nothing changes. The entitlement_grant store keeps one row per account, entitlement, and source, so two active sources both have to be revoked before the entitlement is actually lost. Canceling the subscription revokes only its row; the one-time grant keeps the bundle entitled.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1580:      \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1593:        \"gateStatus + the packument route: the same offline-Ed25519 entitlement check the index Worker uses, gating every `bun install`\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1599:        title: \"Reuses the index Worker's entitlement math, not a second gate\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1626:          \"Every packument and tarball request runs the same offline-Ed25519 entitlement check the free index already uses. An unentitled request gets 401 with no token or 404 with the wrong one; it never gets the package.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:1641:      \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:2089:      \"An MCP server (Model Context Protocol server) exposes tools an AI coding agent can call directly over a standard transport, instead of a bespoke per-agent integration. Caisson's buyer-facing MCP server authenticates every call with a timing-safe Bearer token, gates each tool to the caller's owned entitlements, rate-limits per account, and lets an agent generate a licensed project without a browser.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:2102:        title: \"Constant-time entitlement gate\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:2103:        body: \"isEntitled() scans every owned entitlement with no early return and compares each one timing-safe, so a bundle tool a buyer does not own renders the identical 404 a nonexistent tool would, never leaking which is which.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:2124:          \"Every tool call re-checks the caller's owned entitlements against the tool's required entitlement in constant time; a tool you do not own is excluded from list_tools and returns the same 404 a nonexistent tool would, so ownership is never leaked by the error itself.\",\napps/site/lib/glossary.tsx:2967:      \"software-entitlement\",\napps/site/lib/audit-chain-sample.ts:25:      event: \"entitlement.granted\",\napps/site/lib/subscription-cancel.test.ts:1:// ADR-0293 G14: ownership-check + Paddle-call orchestration. `CancelSubscriptionDeps` carries only a\napps/site/lib/subscription-cancel.test.ts:3:// to inject, which is itself the proof this module cannot touch entitlement_grant/subscription_status\napps/site/lib/subscription-cancel.test.ts:22:test(\"cancels an owned, active subscription and returns Paddle's scheduled-change result\", async () => {\napps/site/lib/members-gate.ts:1:// The org-controls entitlement gate for /dashboard/members (ADR-0257 §1.3). The member-management\napps/site/lib/members-gate.ts:3:// entitlement to reach it. Mirrors the plan page's entitlement-read pattern (readEntitlementGrants →\napps/site/lib/members-gate.ts:14:import { activeEntitlementIds } from \"./active-entitlements.ts\";\napps/site/lib/members-gate.ts:17: * True only when `accountId` holds an ACTIVE entitlement covering org-controls: the module itself,\napps/site/lib/session-hint-cookie.ts:2:// zero-dependency module so `app/api/cart/owned/route.ts` (which reads the cookie directly via\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.ts:1:// The AI-Production entitlement gate for /dashboard/ai-keys (CAISSON-64 P1). BYOK (bring-your-own\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.ts:4:// (`packages/registry-schema/src/entitlements.ts`'s `EDITION_BUNDLE_ID` maps the legacy `ai-kit`\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.ts:6:// `entitlements` onto `[\"ai-production\"]`, ADR-0270). There is no standalone à-la-carte SKU that\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.ts:14:// active-grant read — previously /dashboard/ai-keys had NO entitlement check at all (reachable by\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.ts:22:import { activeEntitlementIds } from \"./active-entitlements.ts\";\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:1:// The /dashboard/ai-keys entitlement gate (CAISSON-64 P1) over real PGlite RLS. Mirrors\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:31:  entitlementId: string;\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:37:    `INSERT INTO entitlement_grant\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:38:       (id, account_id, entitlement_id, source_kind, purchase_id, source_event_id, status, granted_at, revoked_at)\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:40:       ('${opts.entitlementId}-${opts.status}', '${opts.accountId}', '${opts.entitlementId}',\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:41:        'one_time', 'pi_${opts.entitlementId}', 'evt_${opts.entitlementId}', '${opts.status}', now(), ${revokedAt});`,\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:51:  test(\"DENY: only a different active entitlement\", async () => {\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:55:      entitlementId: \"compliance-core\",\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:65:      entitlementId: \"credits\",\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:75:      entitlementId: \"ai-production\",\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:85:      entitlementId: \"ai-production\",\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:95:      entitlementId: \"everything\",\napps/site/lib/ai-production-gate.test.ts:105:      entitlementId: \"ai-production\",\napps/site/lib/layout.shared.tsx:20:// monospace caisson / docs); the theme is owned by the site, not fumadocs (ADR-0042/0045).\napps/site/lib/base-substrate.ts:93:    body: \"Provider-agnostic AI configuration and a Model Context Protocol server that treats agents as principals: timing-safe Bearer auth, tools invisible outside the caller's entitlements, a per-account rate limit on every dispatch. Most kits ship an MCP server now — the question is what it lets an agent do. The design-system contracts an agent reasons over — the component manifest, its typed reader, the contrast and static-usage checkers — are open source too, in @caisson/ds-manifest.\",\napps/site/lib/deploy-migrate.ts:6://  1. PLATFORM schema — the `app` RLS role + every FORCE-RLS platform table (credits, entitlement,\napps/site/lib/ask-ai/spend.ts:7:// anonymous; the premium lane's session is not entitlement-scoped here), so this is a pair of global\napps/site/lib/ask-ai/spend.ts:22:/** The per-lane daily spend counters. No RLS: a non-tenant, app-owned aggregate the route reads/writes\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:199:          \"ShipFast is a one-time $199 (Starter) or $249 (All-in) with lifetime updates, verified 2026-07-07. Caisson is a one-time perpetual license per bundle or module with 12 months of updates included, renewable per entitlement afterward. See the licensing section below for the live figures.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:279:          \"Yes. Both are one-time perpetual licenses you own the source of. MakerKit is $299 (1 dev) or $599 (up to 5), verified 2026-07-07, with continuous updates. Caisson is one-time per bundle or module with 12 months of updates included and per-entitlement renewal after. See the licensing section for live figures.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1147:          \"Vanta is an annual subscription that renews for as long as you need to stay audit-ready; its live site lists no public self-serve price and is quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (see the licensing section for the live figure) and you own the source. They cover different costs: rented monitoring versus owned controls.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1231:          \"Drata is an annual subscription with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (the live figure is in the licensing section) where you own the source. One rents automation of your program; the other is the owned controls underneath it.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1274:        body: \"Secureframe delivers framework coverage as a subscription service; Caisson delivers SOC 2 / HIPAA / EU AI Act mappings as code with OSCAL export, one-time and owned. It does not manage your personnel, vendors, or auditor: it is the earlier, implemented layer.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1320:          \"Secureframe is an annual subscription with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (see the licensing section for the live figure) with the source owned. One rents program management; the other is the owned controls underneath.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1361:        title: \"One-time and owned, not a subscription operator\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1404:          \"Sprinto is an annual subscription with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (the live figure is in the licensing section) with the source owned. One rents a running program; the other is the owned controls it runs on.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1445:        title: \"Depth of implementation, owned one-time\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1446:        body: \"Scytale sells framework breadth and expert service as a subscription; Caisson ships the depth (the actual RLS, audit, and evidence code) one-time and owned. It does not run pentests, provide GRC experts, or manage your auditor; it is the implemented layer beneath.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1492:          \"Scytale is an annual subscription with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (see the licensing section for the live figure) with the source owned. One rents breadth and expertise; the other is the owned depth underneath.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1536:        body: \"Thoropass sells the audit path and evidence collection as a service; Caisson ships the evidence pipeline itself (the code that produces byte-stable OSCAL packs) one-time and owned. It does not perform your audit or sign your report; it is the implemented layer the auditor inspects.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1582:          \"Thoropass is a subscription plus an audit engagement, with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (the live figure is in the licensing section) with the source owned. One is the audit path as a service; the other is the owned controls the audit examines.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1629:        body: \"Caisson is one-time and owned, not a subscription: it does not monitor your org or manage your auditor, and its evidence packs are versioned in your repo where they can be re-generated and re-verified at any time.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1686:          \"Delve is an annual subscription with no public self-serve price, quote-based as of 2026-07-07. Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license (see the licensing section for the live figure) with the source owned. One rents AI-driven collection; the other is the owned controls that produce the evidence.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1703:      \"AuditKit is a subscription audit-log SDK plus SOC 2 prep platform (hash-chained logs, evidence vault, $99–999+/mo); Caisson is the owned compliance substrate (externally anchored WORM chain, field encryption, deterministic OSCAL evidence packs), one-time source you own. An honest, dated comparison.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1803:          \"On the tamper-evident audit-log and compliance-evidence wedge, yes, with a different model: AuditKit is a subscription service (SDK + managed API + prep workflow), Caisson is source-owned infrastructure. On the GRC-workflow features (access reviews, vendor tracking, policy templates, auditor portal), no: AuditKit ships those and Caisson doesn't; Caisson's substitute for the portal is a signed, downloadable evidence pack.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1813:          \"AuditKit publishes $99–$999+/mo subscription tiers, metered by event volume (auditkit.dev, 2026-07-10). Caisson's Compliance bundle is a one-time perpetual license per organization (see the licensing section for the live figure) with 12 months of updates included and the source yours either way. One is a metered service; the other is owned infrastructure.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1818:  // The closest philosophical comparable — both open-source, dev-owned. The honest distinction is\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1832:      \"Comp AI is an open-source GRC platform you run. Caisson is an Apache-2.0 library that implements the controls a platform inspects. Both are dev-owned: here is the honest line.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1846:        body: \"Comp AI is a real, capable compliance platform whose code is open and self-hostable at no license fee: AI agents, 580+ integrations, continuous monitoring, and a Trust Center. For a team that wants to run its own dev-owned compliance program, that is a strong, honest offering Caisson does not replicate.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1890:      \"You want the implemented controls (RLS with isolation tests, a WORM audit trail, and OSCAL evidence) as an Apache-2.0 library composed into your app, one-time and owned.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1892:      \"Self-host Comp AI to run the program and use Caisson to implement the controls in your app: a fully dev-owned stack where Caisson emits the evidence Comp AI monitors and presents.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1897:          \"Partly, but they are different form factors. Comp AI is an open-source GRC platform you deploy to monitor your stack and run the audit workflow; Caisson is an infrastructure library you compose into the application itself. Caisson doesn't run continuous monitoring or a Trust Center, and Comp AI doesn't ship the RLS, audit chain, or field encryption that live inside your app. Many dev-owned teams would use both.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1922:      \"Pick Probo to run a compliance program: an MIT-licensed, self-hostable GRC platform (risk register, control library with a Statement of Applicability, vendor risk, access reviews, audit programs, 270+ MCP tools) or its 'Compliance, Done For You' service, where dedicated compliance officers run the program and manage your auditor. Pick Caisson for the other half: the technical controls inside your application, owned one-time — fail-closed Postgres RLS with isolation tests, a WORM + hash-chained audit trail, per-tenant field encryption, and OSCAL evidence packs. Probo tracks that a control exists; Caisson is the control.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:1993:      \"You need the technical controls implemented and provable inside your own application, one-time and owned, with the program run by your team or by someone else.\",\napps/site/lib/comparisons.ts:2113:      \"You need the controls themselves to exist at runtime — fail-closed tenant isolation the database enforces, a WORM-anchored audit trail, and evidence generated from the live system — in TypeScript on Postgres, owned one-time.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
