{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba .github/workflows/publish-image.yml'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# T28 reference: image publication (doc 07 §3 + §4).\n     2\t#\n     3\t# COPY THIS FILE VERBATIM into each app repo as `.github/workflows/publish-image.yml`.\n     4\t# Everything repo-specific is a variable or lives in `deploy/services.json`, so the four\n     5\t# copies stay byte-identical and drift is detectable by comparing hashes. If you find\n     6\t# yourself editing the body for one repo, that is a bug in this template — fix it here.\n     7\t#\n     8\t# What it is NOT: a deployer. Publishing and deploying are separate identities (doc 07\n     9\t# §2) and separate workflows on purpose. This job may write to Artifact Registry and\n    10\t# nothing else; it holds no Cloud Run permission at all.\n    11\t\n    12\tname: publish image\n    13\t\n    14\ton:\n    15\t  push:\n    16\t    branches: [main]\n    17\t  workflow_dispatch:\n    18\t    inputs:\n    19\t      service:\n    20\t        description: \"Single service key to publish; default is every service this repo owns\"\n    21\t        required: false\n    22\t        type: string\n    23\t  workflow_call:\n    24\t    inputs:\n    25\t      service:\n    26\t        required: false\n    27\t        type: string\n    28\t    outputs:\n    29\t      digests:\n    30\t        description: JSON object mapping service key to the published image digest\n    31\t        value: ${{ jobs.collect.outputs.digests }}\n    32\t\n    33\t# doc 07 §1: never two publishes of the same ref at once — the second would race the\n    34\t# first's provenance attestation onto a digest it did not build.\n    35\tconcurrency:\n    36\t  group: publish-${{ github.ref }}\n    37\t  cancel-in-progress: false\n    38\t\n    39\tpermissions:\n    40\t  contents: read\n    41\t\n    42\tjobs:\n    43\t  # Which services does this push actually change? `watched_paths` in the canonical\n    44\t  # manifest answers exactly that question (doc 07 §4), so it is the matrix source —\n    45\t  # never a hand-maintained list that drifts from the manifest the review approved.\n    46\t  select:\n    47\t    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n    48\t    outputs:\n    49\t      matrix: ${{ steps.pick.outputs.matrix }}\n    50\t      any: ${{ steps.pick.outputs.any }}\n    51\t    steps:\n    52\t      # An unset REPOSITORY VARIABLE interpolates to the empty string. It does not fail, and\n    53\t      # GitHub — not this template — owns what \"empty\" then means, so an absent variable is a\n    54\t      # value chosen by someone who is not in the room.\n    55\t      #\n    56\t      # `BUN_VERSION_FILE` is the one that proves this is not hygiene. Read at the pinned\n    57\t      # setup-bun SHA (`0c5077e5`, `action.yml`: neither `bun-version` nor `bun-version-file`\n    58\t      # is required, the latter defaults to null; `src/action.ts:158`: \"If no version\n    59\t      # specified, default is 'latest'\"). So an empty value does not error — it installs the\n    60\t      # newest Bun, the repo's pin silently stops binding, and the run is green. Same shape\n    61\t      # as the missing dependency install: a success nobody had to produce.\n    62\t      #\n    63\t      # Asserted for every variable the job reads rather than only the ones argued to be\n    64\t      # dangerous, because deciding which ones self-report is exactly the reasoning that\n    65\t      # produced the gap. Each job lists its own; a job that stops reading one drops it here\n    66\t      # in the same edit.\n    67\t      #\n    68\t      # First step in the job — before checkout, before `setup-bun`, before any credential.\n    69\t      # A misconfigured repo costs one red run and nothing else.\n    70\t      - name: assert the repository variables are set\n    71\t        env:\n    72\t          BUN_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n    73\t        run: |\n    74\t          set -euo pipefail\n    75\t          missing=\"\"\n    76\t          for v in BUN_VERSION_FILE; do\n    77\t            [ -n \"${!v:-}\" ] || missing=\"$missing $v\"\n    78\t          done\n    79\t          if [ -n \"$missing\" ]; then\n    80\t            echo \"::error::unset repository variables:$missing\"\n    81\t            exit 1\n    82\t          fi\n    83\t\n    84\t      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n    85\t        with:\n    86\t          fetch-depth: 0\n    87\t\n    88\t      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n    89\t        with:\n    90\t          bun-version-file: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n    91\t\n    92\t      # Every `bun run deploy/*.ts` in this pipeline resolves this repo's dependencies, and\n    93\t      # until 2026-08-21 nothing installed them. Bun's auto-install covered that silently:\n    94\t      # it fetches from the registry against package.json RANGES, not `bun.lock`, which\n    95\t      # made the deploy path the only place in these repos where the lockfile did not bind.\n    96\t      # Every other workflow in all four repos runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile`; the\n    97\t      # T28 template was the exception, and that was an oversight, not a decision.\n    98\t      #\n    99\t      # gridwork is the one repo with nothing to install: it has no root `package.json`\n   100\t      # (its Bun project is `site/`) and its `deploy/*.ts` are deliberately\n   101\t      # dependency-free. The `exit 1` arm is what keeps that exemption honest — a repo that\n   102\t      # grows a package import without a root lockfile fails here instead of quietly\n   103\t      # auto-installing. `node:` and `bun:` specifiers are builtins, installed from nowhere.\n   104\t      #\n   105\t      # This step is byte-identical in all four workflows. Copy it, do not adapt it.\n   106\t      - name: install the deploy scripts' dependencies\n   107\t        run: |\n   108\t          set -euo pipefail\n   109\t          if [ -f bun.lock ]; then\n   110\t            bun install --frozen-lockfile\n   111\t          elif grep -hoE 'from \"[^\"]+\"' deploy/*.ts | grep -qvE 'from \"(\\.|node:|bun:)'; then\n   112\t            echo \"::error::deploy/*.ts imports a package but this repo has no root bun.lock\"\n   113\t            exit 1\n   114\t          fi\n   115\t\n   116\t      - id: pick\n   117\t        env:\n   118\t          ONLY: ${{ inputs.service }}\n   119\t          BEFORE: ${{ github.event.before }}\n   120\t        run: bun run deploy/select-services.ts >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   121\t\n   122\t  publish:\n   123\t    needs: select\n   124\t    if: needs.select.outputs.any == 'true'\n   125\t    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n   126\t    timeout-minutes: 45\n   127\t    strategy:\n   128\t      fail-fast: false\n   129\t      matrix:\n   130\t        service: ${{ fromJSON(needs.select.outputs.matrix) }}\n   131\t    permissions:\n   132\t      contents: read\n   133\t      # doc 07 §1 rule 3: OIDC/WIF only, never a JSON key. The same token is what cosign\n   134\t      # trades for its signing certificate below — one grant, two consumers, no key anywhere.\n   135\t      # `attestations: write` is gone with the attestation API (see \"sign the digest\").\n   136\t      id-token: write\n   137\t    steps:\n   138\t      # doc 07 §3: a fork PR gets nothing. This workflow is push/dispatch-only so a fork\n   139\t      # cannot reach it, but say so out loud rather than relying on the trigger list\n   140\t      # staying that way — the failure mode of a widened trigger is silent credential\n   141\t      # exposure, and this check costs one step.\n   142\t      - name: refuse a fork ref\n   143\t        if: github.event.pull_request.head.repo.fork == true\n   144\t        run: |\n   145\t          echo \"::error::fork refs never publish images (doc 07 §3)\"\n   146\t          exit 1\n   147\t\n   148\t      # See the `select` job for why an unset repository variable is a vendor-chosen value\n   149\t      # rather than a failure. This job reads five; it asserts all five.\n   150\t      - name: assert the repository variables are set\n   151\t        env:\n   152\t          BUN_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n   153\t          GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA: ${{ vars.GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA }}\n   154\t          GCP_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n   155\t          GCP_REGISTRY_HOST: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY_HOST }}\n   156\t          GCP_WIF_PROVIDER: ${{ vars.GCP_WIF_PROVIDER }}\n   157\t          NODE_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.NODE_VERSION_FILE }}\n   158\t        run: |\n   159\t          set -euo pipefail\n   160\t          missing=\"\"\n   161\t          for v in BUN_VERSION_FILE GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA GCP_REGISTRY GCP_REGISTRY_HOST GCP_WIF_PROVIDER NODE_VERSION_FILE; do\n   162\t            [ -n \"${!v:-}\" ] || missing=\"$missing $v\"\n   163\t          done\n   164\t          if [ -n \"$missing\" ]; then\n   165\t            echo \"::error::unset repository variables:$missing\"\n   166\t            exit 1\n   167\t          fi\n   168\t\n   169\t      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n   170\t\n   171\t      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n   172\t        with:\n   173\t          bun-version-file: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n   174\t\n   175\t      # CAISSON-209 (2026-08-25, caisson run 32904702863): `deploy/gates.sh` shells Node\n   176\t      # tools — dependency-cruiser's floor is ^22||^24||>=26 — and a Bun-only job runs\n   177\t      # Node SHIMS on whatever Node the runner image ships (20.20.0 that day). With no\n   178\t      # pin there was nothing to drift and nothing to report until a tool raised its\n   179\t      # floor. Same discipline as BUN_VERSION_FILE: the version lives in a repo-owned pin\n   180\t      # file named by a repository variable asserted above — never a literal here that\n   181\t      # drifts per repo — and an EMPTY file input falls back to the same system Node this\n   182\t      # exists to escape, which is why the variable is in the assertion.\n   183\t      - uses: actions/setup-node@820762786026740c76f36085b0efc47a31fe5020 # v7.0.0\n   184\t        with:\n   185\t          node-version-file: ${{ vars.NODE_VERSION_FILE }}\n   186\t\n   187\t      # Same step as the `select` job above — see there for why it is not an unconditional\n   188\t      # `bun install` and what the `exit 1` arm catches. The bodies must not drift.\n   189\t      - name: install the deploy scripts' dependencies\n   190\t        run: |\n   191\t          set -euo pipefail\n   192\t          if [ -f bun.lock ]; then\n   193\t            bun install --frozen-lockfile\n   194\t          elif grep -hoE 'from \"[^\"]+\"' deploy/*.ts | grep -qvE 'from \"(\\.|node:|bun:)'; then\n   195\t            echo \"::error::deploy/*.ts imports a package but this repo has no root bun.lock\"\n   196\t            exit 1\n   197\t          fi\n   198\t\n   199\t      - name: resolve build inputs\n   200\t        id: svc\n   201\t        env:\n   202\t          SERVICE: ${{ matrix.service }}\n   203\t        run: bun run deploy/service-inputs.ts >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   204\t\n   205\t      # doc 07 §3: lint, typecheck, tests, and an image that builds — before anything is\n   206\t      # pushed anywhere. A digest that was never tested is worse than no digest, because\n   207\t      # the rollback ladder will happily promote it.\n   208\t      #\n   209\t      # The commands live in the repo's own `deploy/gates.sh`, not here. They were literal\n   210\t      # `bun run lint/typecheck/test` until the gridwork adoption proved that wrong: that\n   211\t      # repo has no root package.json, pins Bun at site/package.json, and runs its lint\n   212\t      # through ./tools/lint.sh. Four repos do not share a script vocabulary, and a\n   213\t      # template that assumes they do forces exactly the per-repo body edit this design\n   214\t      # exists to prevent. `gates.sh` must exit non-zero when any gate fails.\n   215\t      - name: gates\n   216\t        run: bash deploy/gates.sh\n   217\t\n   218\t      # Anything that must happen on the HOST before Docker is handed the build\n   219\t      # context. dev-profile is why this exists: its Dockerfile builds a staged\n   220\t      # public-only tree (tools/build-site-target.ts -> .site-build-public/) behind a\n   221\t      # .dockerignore allowlist, so the staging step cannot move inside the Dockerfile —\n   222\t      # by the time Docker runs, the context has already been filtered.\n   223\t      #\n   224\t      # REQUIRED in every repo, not skip-if-absent. A repo with nothing to prepare ships\n   225\t      # a one-line script saying so. An `if [ -f ... ]` guard here would make \"no\n   226\t      # preparation needed\" and \"the script is missing\" identical at a glance, and the\n   227\t      # failure mode of the second is an image built from the wrong tree that passes\n   228\t      # every gate above it.\n   229\t      - name: prepare build context\n   230\t        run: bash deploy/prepare-build.sh\n   231\t\n   232\t      - id: auth\n   233\t        uses: google-github-actions/auth@7c6bc770dae815cd3e89ee6cdf493a5fab2cc093 # v3.0.0\n   234\t        with:\n   235\t          workload_identity_provider: ${{ vars.GCP_WIF_PROVIDER }}\n   236\t          service_account: ${{ vars.GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA }}\n   237\t          token_format: access_token\n   238\t          # doc 07 §4: the generated credential file must never reach a Docker context.\n   239\t          # `create_credentials_file: false` is the mechanical guarantee; .dockerignore\n   240\t          # entries are the belt, this is the braces.\n   241\t          create_credentials_file: false\n   242\t\n   243\t      # `GCP_REGISTRY_HOST` is the host `docker login` sends the access token to, and\n   244\t      # `GCP_REGISTRY` is the host the image is actually pushed to. They are two\n   245\t      # independent repository variables that only happen to agree, and nothing checked\n   246\t      # that they did — so a principal who can write repository variables but cannot read\n   247\t      # secrets (a `repo`-scoped PAT, an org owner setting an org-level var) could point\n   248\t      # the login at a host they control and receive a live GCP access token for the\n   249\t      # image-publisher SA. The push to GCP_REGISTRY then fails and the run goes red,\n   250\t      # after the credential has already left. `create_credentials_file: false` does not\n   251\t      # help: it stops the credential reaching a Docker CONTEXT, not a login to the wrong\n   252\t      # host.\n   253\t      #\n   254\t      # Asserted rather than derived because `docker login` takes a host, not a\n   255\t      # host-plus-path, so GCP_REGISTRY cannot simply be passed through. The pair check is\n   256\t      # the property that matters: the token only ever goes to the host the image goes to.\n   257\t      - name: assert the login host is the registry's own host\n   258\t        env:\n   259\t          REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n   260\t          REGISTRY_HOST: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY_HOST }}\n   261\t        run: |\n   262\t          set -euo pipefail\n   263\t          case \"$REGISTRY_HOST\" in\n   264\t            *.pkg.dev) ;;\n   265\t            *) echo \"::error::GCP_REGISTRY_HOST is not an Artifact Registry host\"; exit 1 ;;\n   266\t          esac\n   267\t          case \"$REGISTRY\" in\n   268\t            \"$REGISTRY_HOST\"/*) ;;\n   269\t            *) echo \"::error::GCP_REGISTRY_HOST is not the host of GCP_REGISTRY — refusing to send a registry credential to it\"; exit 1 ;;\n   270\t          esac\n   271\t\n   272\t      - uses: docker/login-action@dbcb813823bdd20940b903addbd779551569679f # v4.6.0\n   273\t        with:\n   274\t          registry: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY_HOST }}\n   275\t          username: oauth2accesstoken\n   276\t          password: ${{ steps.auth.outputs.access_token }}\n   277\t\n   278\t      - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@37fe631027851001ddb9b187196cc803df7f5f0e # v4.3.0\n   279\t\n   280\t      # CORE-73: a workflow_dispatch re-run of an already-published sha used to go RED at\n   281\t      # push time — the rebuild is not byte-reproducible, so the same :sha tag arrives\n   282\t      # with a new manifest and Artifact Registry's tag immutability refuses it. A red\n   283\t      # that reads as a regression while saying only \"this was already published\". The\n   284\t      # registry's existing digest IS the truth for that sha, so a re-run re-emits it and\n   285\t      # skips the build instead of failing. It is the truth about WHAT the bytes are —\n   286\t      # not about whether they were signed or scanned: the run that pushed may have died\n   287\t      # between the push and either claim, so the evidence chain below keys on the\n   288\t      # resolved digest (see `resolve digest`) and re-earns both on every run.\n   289\t      #\n   290\t      # Three outcomes, fail-closed: the tag resolves -> published=true plus its digest;\n   291\t      # the registry says the tag/repo does not exist -> published=false, build proceeds;\n   292\t      # anything else (auth, network, parse) -> red. \"Cannot tell\" must never build —\n   293\t      # guessing \"not published\" here would recreate the immutability red this exists to\n   294\t      # remove, one step later and with less context. `imagetools inspect` reads through\n   295\t      # the docker login above and returns the same index digest buildx emitted when it\n   296\t      # pushed, which is what every deploy and rollback consumes.\n   297\t      - name: already published at this sha?\n   298\t        id: preflight\n   299\t        env:\n   300\t          IMAGE: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}\n   301\t          TAG: ${{ github.sha }}\n   302\t        run: |\n   303\t          set -euo pipefail\n   304\t          if out=\"$(docker buildx imagetools inspect \"${IMAGE}:${TAG}\" --format '{{json .Manifest}}' 2>preflight-err.txt)\"; then\n   305\t            digest=\"$(printf '%s' \"$out\" | jq -r '.digest // empty')\"\n   306\t            case \"$digest\" in\n   307\t              sha256:?*) ;;\n   308\t              *) echo \"::error::registry answered for ${IMAGE}:${TAG} but returned no parseable digest\"; exit 1 ;;\n   309\t            esac\n   310\t            printf 'published=true\\ndigest=%s\\n' \"$digest\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   311\t            echo \"::notice title=already published (CORE-73)::${IMAGE}:${TAG}@${digest} — build skipped, digest re-emitted\"\n   312\t          elif grep -qiE 'not found|NAME_UNKNOWN|MANIFEST_UNKNOWN' preflight-err.txt; then\n   313\t            printf 'published=false\\n' >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   314\t          else\n   315\t            echo \"::error::cannot determine whether ${IMAGE}:${TAG} is already published — refusing to guess\"\n   316\t            cat preflight-err.txt >&2\n   317\t            exit 1\n   318\t          fi\n   319\t\n   320\t      # No `latest`. Doc 07 §4: human-readable tags may carry the full SHA, but nothing\n   321\t      # in a deploy or a rollback ever resolves a tag — they consume the digest this step\n   322\t      # emits. A floating tag in a rollback path is how you roll back to the thing you\n   323\t      # were trying to escape.\n   324\t      #\n   325\t      # Skipped when preflight found the tag (CORE-73). The evidence chain below does NOT\n   326\t      # skip with it: sign, SBOM, scan, manifest, and the evidence upload key on the\n   327\t      # resolved digest, not on this step — evidence follows the digest, whichever run\n   328\t      # pushed it (see `resolve digest`).\n   329\t      - name: build and push by digest\n   330\t        id: build\n   331\t        if: steps.preflight.outputs.published != 'true'\n   332\t        uses: docker/build-push-action@53b7df96c91f9c12dcc8a07bcb9ccacbed38856a # v7.3.0\n   333\t        with:\n   334\t          context: ${{ steps.svc.outputs.build_context }}\n   335\t          file: ${{ steps.svc.outputs.dockerfile }}\n   336\t          push: true\n   337\t          provenance: mode=max\n   338\t          sbom: true\n   339\t          tags: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}:${{ github.sha }}\n   340\t          build-args: |\n   341\t            GIT_SHA=${{ github.sha }}\n   342\t          labels: |\n   343\t            org.opencontainers.image.revision=${{ github.sha }}\n   344\t            org.opencontainers.image.source=${{ github.server_url }}/${{ github.repository }}\n   345\t\n   346\t      # One digest, one fact. `steps.build.outcome == 'success'` used to key all five\n   347\t      # attestation steps below (sign, SBOM, scan, manifest, evidence upload) — a proxy\n   348\t      # for three different facts (a digest exists / it is signed / it is scanned) that\n   349\t      # went wrong on the CORE-73 skip path: a skipped build's outcome is 'skipped', so\n   350\t      # all five guards skipped with it, `record digest` re-emitted the preflight digest\n   351\t      # ungated, and the re-run went green carrying an unearned \"already signed, already\n   352\t      # scanned\" claim. Two live shapes of that hole: a TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1 repo whose run\n   353\t      # 1 pushed, signed, then went red on a CRITICAL — \"Re-run failed jobs\" published\n   354\t      # the blocked digest green, with production's cosign verify passing because run 1\n   355\t      # genuinely signed; and a sign that died after the push, whose digest no later run\n   356\t      # could ever sign — every re-run entered the skip path and skipped sign with it.\n   357\t      #\n   358\t      # This step resolves THE digest — the build's, or the one preflight read back —\n   359\t      # and everything below keys on its existence: `always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''`.\n   360\t      # A skipped step's outputs are empty strings, so a failed build still skips the\n   361\t      # chain and a red preflight still reds the job: fail-closed unchanged. On the skip\n   362\t      # path the run re-EARNS the claims instead of inheriting them: cosign sign is\n   363\t      # idempotent (one more Rekor entry, same verification), the SBOM and the scan read\n   364\t      # the digest from the registry by ref — no rebuild — and a repo that set\n   365\t      # TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1 keeps its gate: a re-run of a scan-blocked publish re-scans\n   366\t      # and goes red again. The deliberate cost: with TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1, a re-dispatch\n   367\t      # of an old green sha can go red on a CVE published since — that is the repo's own\n   368\t      # policy speaking, not a liveness failure; the unset-default ('0') re-dispatch\n   369\t      # stays green and keeps the liveness-probe property.\n   370\t      - name: resolve digest\n   371\t        id: image\n   372\t        env:\n   373\t          # Whichever path ran: the build's digest, or the one preflight read back from\n   374\t          # the registry (CORE-73). Exactly one is ever non-empty.\n   375\t          DIGEST: ${{ steps.build.outputs.digest || steps.preflight.outputs.digest }}\n   376\t        run: |\n   377\t          set -euo pipefail\n   378\t          printf 'digest=%s\\n' \"$DIGEST\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n   379\t\n   380\t      # doc 07 §4 asks for provenance on the published digest. This was\n   381\t      # `actions/attest-build-provenance`, and on three of the four repos it failed AFTER\n   382\t      # the push: the GitHub attestations API is plan-gated for private repositories, so\n   383\t      # the step went red with the digest already in the registry, `collect` was skipped,\n   384\t      # and `digests` never published — a good build made unshippable by a billing tier.\n   385\t      # Operator ruling R44 (2026-08-25): sign with Sigstore directly, no GitHub API in\n   386\t      # the path.\n   387\t      #\n   388\t      # Keyless. cosign trades this job's OIDC token for a short-lived Fulcio certificate\n   389\t      # whose subject IS this workflow file at this ref, signs the digest, and records the\n   390\t      # signature in the public Rekor log. There is no key to hold, rotate, or leak, and\n   391\t      # the verifier — deploy-production.yml, the one place these digests become\n   392\t      # production revisions — checks an identity STRING, not a key it has to fetch: a\n   393\t      # digest that this workflow at refs/heads/main did not sign is refused there before\n   394\t      # any revision exists. A key-based alternative (a KMS key) would trade that for new\n   395\t      # infra, IAM on two identities, and a verifier that must hold a credential.\n   396\t      #\n   397\t      # Residual, stated rather than implied: the Rekor entry is public and names this\n   398\t      # repository, this workflow path, the commit, and the image reference (registry\n   399\t      # host, GCP project, service). Nothing secret — but a private repo's name and its\n   400\t      # registry path become discoverable. Accepted by the ruling.\n   401\t      #\n   402\t      # Signed by DIGEST: a tag is a pointer, and signing the pointer proves nothing about\n   403\t      # what it points at today. `--yes` is the non-interactive consent to the Rekor\n   404\t      # upload. The pinned `cosign-release` must match deploy-production.yml's — the two\n   405\t      # are one number in two files, and the template test holds them equal.\n   406\t      - uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@6f9f17788090df1f26f669e9d70d6ae9567deba6 # v4.1.2\n   407\t        with:\n   408\t          cosign-release: v3.1.3\n   409\t\n   410\t      - name: sign the digest (keyless)\n   411\t        if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''\n   412\t        env:\n   413\t          IMAGE: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}\n   414\t          DIGEST: ${{ steps.image.outputs.digest }}\n   415\t        run: |\n   416\t          set -euo pipefail\n   417\t          cosign sign --yes \"${IMAGE}@${DIGEST}\"\n   418\t\n   419\t      # Guarded like the manifest below (see there): once the digest exists, the SBOM and\n   420\t      # the scan run whatever failed between the build and here. Under the default\n   421\t      # `success()` a failed sign skipped both, so the run ended with a live digest and a\n   422\t      # CVE list it could not show — studio's report measured exactly that shape.\n   423\t      - name: sbom\n   424\t        if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''\n   425\t        uses: anchore/sbom-action@3ad7283483fc7af8ff2b4ea19663c2d5ca935e26 # v0.24.2\n   426\t        with:\n   427\t          image: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}@${{ steps.image.outputs.digest }}\n   428\t          artifact-name: sbom-${{ matrix.service }}.spdx.json\n   429\t          output-file: sbom-${{ matrix.service }}.spdx.json\n   430\t\n   431\t      - name: vulnerability scan\n   432\t        if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''\n   433\t        uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@ed142fd0673e97e23eac54620cfb913e5ce36c25 # v0.36.0\n   434\t        with:\n   435\t          image-ref: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}@${{ steps.image.outputs.digest }}\n   436\t          format: sarif\n   437\t          output: trivy-${{ matrix.service }}.sarif\n   438\t          # Report by default, gate by repo policy (CORE-74) — a template that blocks\n   439\t          # every publish on a base-image CVE with no fix available gets disabled within\n   440\t          # a week, and a disabled scan is worth less than a recorded one. So the dial is\n   441\t          # a repository variable, never a workflow edit: unset resolves to '0' (record\n   442\t          # in SARIF, never block); a repo that wants CRITICAL,HIGH findings to fail the\n   443\t          # publish sets TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1. Deliberately absent from the assert loop —\n   444\t          # unset is the documented default, not a misconfiguration.\n   445\t          exit-code: ${{ vars.TRIVY_EXIT_CODE || '0' }}\n   446\t          severity: CRITICAL,HIGH\n   447\t\n   448\t      # doc 07 §11: the evidence IS the deliverable. A deploy that cannot show its\n   449\t      # digest, its tests and its scan is not a deploy you can reason about at 3am.\n   450\t      #\n   451\t      # Every value arrives through `env:` and is read as \"$VAR\" — including the output\n   452\t      # FILENAME. This was nine ${{ }} interpolations inside a `cat <<JSON` heredoc, the\n   453\t      # same defect class as the confirmed deploy-staging exploit: the runner substitutes\n   454\t      # ${{ }} into the script TEXT before a shell parses it, so a value containing $(...)\n   455\t      # arrived as executable source, after auth, with a live registry credential in\n   456\t      # reach. Quoting the delimiter is not the fix — a value carrying a quote still\n   457\t      # breaks out of the surrounding quoting, and one carrying the delimiter line still\n   458\t      # ends the heredoc early. env: is the fix: the value goes to the process\n   459\t      # environment, never into the script text.\n   460\t      #\n   461\t      # \"Those inputs are hard to reach\" is not a security property — `dockerfile` and\n   462\t      # `build_context` come from deploy/service-inputs.ts reading deploy/services.json,\n   463\t      # generated from a manifest a human edits. `jq -n --arg` builds the body for the\n   464\t      # same reason: a quote or backslash yields valid JSON, not a file collect cannot\n   465\t      # parse. No -c, deliberately — this file is read by a human, and the -c rule binds\n   466\t      # values captured into a variable or written to $GITHUB_OUTPUT.\n   467\t      #\n   468\t      # `if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''`, on the sign, SBOM, and scan\n   469\t      # above, here, and on the evidence upload below (2026-08-25, measured on studio run\n   470\t      # 32904680804 and dev-profile's red main; re-keyed 2026-08-26, see `resolve\n   471\t      # digest`): once a digest EXISTS in the registry — this run's build, or a prior\n   472\t      # run's read back by the preflight — the evidence runs, whatever fails around it.\n   473\t      # Under the default `success()` a failed attest, scan, or SBOM skipped them and\n   474\t      # the run ended with a live digest and no manifest, no SBOM, no SARIF — a CVE the\n   475\t      # run could not name, and a digest that had to be re-scanned by hand. Keyed to the\n   476\t      # DIGEST, not to any one predecessor: keying it to the scan is right for a repo\n   477\t      # whose scan fails and wrong for one whose attest fails, and keying it to the\n   478\t      # build was wrong for the run whose build correctly SKIPPED. `collect` is still\n   479\t      # gated on the whole job succeeding, so nothing downstream consumes a digest from\n   480\t      # a failed publish; this only keeps the evidence of what was pushed.\n   481\t      - name: source-to-image manifest\n   482\t        if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''\n   483\t        env:\n   484\t          SERVICE: ${{ matrix.service }}\n   485\t          REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}\n   486\t          COMMIT: ${{ github.sha }}\n   487\t          REF: ${{ github.ref }}\n   488\t          RUN_ID: ${{ github.run_id }}\n   489\t          IMAGE: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}\n   490\t          DIGEST: ${{ steps.image.outputs.digest }}\n   491\t          DOCKERFILE: ${{ steps.svc.outputs.dockerfile }}\n   492\t          BUILD_CONTEXT: ${{ steps.svc.outputs.build_context }}\n   493\t        run: |\n   494\t          set -euo pipefail\n   495\t          jq -n \\\n   496\t            --arg service \"$SERVICE\" \\\n   497\t            --arg repository \"$REPOSITORY\" \\\n   498\t            --arg commit \"$COMMIT\" \\\n   499\t            --arg ref \"$REF\" \\\n   500\t            --arg run_id \"$RUN_ID\" \\\n   501\t            --arg image \"$IMAGE\" \\\n   502\t            --arg digest \"$DIGEST\" \\\n   503\t            --arg dockerfile \"$DOCKERFILE\" \\\n   504\t            --arg build_context \"$BUILD_CONTEXT\" \\\n   505\t            '{service: $service, repository: $repository, commit: $commit, ref: $ref, run_id: $run_id, image: $image, digest: $digest, dockerfile: $dockerfile, build_context: $build_context}' \\\n   506\t            > \"image-manifest-$SERVICE.json\"\n   507\t\n   508\t      # Same guard as the manifest step. The manifest is the anchor: it exists whenever\n   509\t      # a digest was resolved, so `if-no-files-found: error` still binds — an upload with\n   510\t      # NOTHING to carry is a bug, an upload missing the SBOM because the SBOM step failed\n   511\t      # is the evidence of that failure. A `hashFiles` guard would be wrong here: it goes\n   512\t      # quiet on the day the manifest step itself breaks.\n   513\t      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a # v7.0.1\n   514\t        if: always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''\n   515\t        with:\n   516\t          name: publish-evidence-${{ matrix.service }}\n   517\t          path: |\n   518\t            image-manifest-${{ matrix.service }}.json\n   519\t            sbom-${{ matrix.service }}.spdx.json\n   520\t            trivy-${{ matrix.service }}.sarif\n   521\t          if-no-files-found: error\n   522\t          overwrite: true\n   523\t\n   524\t      # Both values through env:. This was `echo '${{ ... }}' > \"digests/${{ ... }}\"` —\n   525\t      # single quotes are not a sandbox, they are text the runner writes around text it\n   526\t      # already substituted, so one quote character in either value ends the quoting and\n   527\t      # the rest of the value is shell. `printf '%s\\n'` also beats `echo` here: echo\n   528\t      # interprets leading dashes and backslashes on some shells, printf does not.\n   529\t      - name: record digest\n   530\t        env:\n   531\t          SERVICE: ${{ matrix.service }}\n   532\t          # The one resolved digest — same value every attestation step above signed,\n   533\t          # SBOM'd, and scanned. Under the default `success()` this step is unreachable\n   534\t          # when any of them failed, so a recorded digest is one whose attestation ran\n   535\t          # and passed ON THIS RUN; the empty-digest refusal below is the belt for the\n   536\t          # pathological build-emitted-nothing path.\n   537\t          DIGEST: ${{ steps.image.outputs.digest }}\n   538\t        run: |\n   539\t          set -euo pipefail\n   540\t          if [ -z \"$DIGEST\" ]; then\n   541\t            echo \"::error::no digest from the build or the preflight — nothing to record\"\n   542\t            exit 1\n   543\t          fi\n   544\t          mkdir -p digests\n   545\t          printf '%s\\n' \"$DIGEST\" > \"digests/$SERVICE\"\n   546\t\n   547\t      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a # v7.0.1\n   548\t        with:\n   549\t          name: digest-${{ matrix.service }}\n   550\t          path: digests/${{ matrix.service }}\n   551\t          if-no-files-found: error\n   552\t          overwrite: true\n   553\t\n   554\t  collect:\n   555\t    needs: publish\n   556\t    if: always() && needs.publish.result == 'success'\n   557\t    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n   558\t    outputs:\n   559\t      digests: ${{ steps.collect.outputs.digests }}\n   560\t    steps:\n   561\t      - uses: actions/download-artifact@3e5f45b2cfb9172054b4087a40e8e0b5a5461e7c # v8.0.1\n   562\t        with:\n   563\t          pattern: digest-*\n   564\t          path: digests\n   565\t          merge-multiple: false\n   566\t      # `jq -c` is load-bearing, not a nicety. Without it jq pretty-prints, so the\n   567\t      # accumulator was MULTILINE from the first service onward and then written with\n   568\t      # `printf 'digests=%s\\n'` into $GITHUB_OUTPUT — a single-line key=value format.\n   569\t      # This was broken, not merely risky: the `digests` workflow_call output was corrupt\n   570\t      # for every non-empty publish, and it is the exact input deploy-staging and\n   571\t      # deploy-production consume. -c keeps the object on one line so the write is valid.\n   572\t      - id: collect\n   573\t        run: |\n   574\t          set -euo pipefail\n   575\t          # nullglob so an empty download expands to nothing rather than to the literal\n   576\t          # pattern. Without it the loop runs once on `digests/digest-*/*`, `cat` fails\n   577\t          # inside a --arg command substitution whose outer jq still exits 0, and the\n   578\t          # output becomes `digests={\"*\":\"\"}` — a green publish handing the deploy\n   579\t          # workflows a service named `*`. Unreachable today (collect is gated on\n   580\t          # publish success and both uploads use if-no-files-found: error), which is\n   581\t          # exactly why it would not be noticed the day one of those changes.\n   582\t          shopt -s nullglob\n   583\t          json='{}'\n   584\t          for d in digests/digest-*/*; do\n   585\t            svc=\"$(basename \"$d\")\"\n   586\t            json=\"$(printf '%s' \"$json\" | jq -c --arg k \"$svc\" --arg v \"$(cat \"$d\")\" '. + {($k): $v}')\"\n   587\t          done\n   588\t          printf 'digests=%s\\n' \"$json\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
