{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_110","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n -C 10 \"bypass suite|smoke|origin gate|Access\" .github/workflows/deploy-staging.yml'","aggregated_output":"1-# T28 reference: stable staging flow (doc 07 §5).\n2-#\n3-# COPY VERBATIM into each app repo as `.github/workflows/deploy-staging.yml`.\n4-#\n5-# The ordering here is the whole point and it is not stylistic:\n6-#\n7-#   publish digest -> record the rollback revision -> update the migration Job TO that\n8:#   digest -> execute the Job -> deploy the same digest to the services -> smoke\n9-#   -> record evidence\n10-#\n11-# The Job definition is updated BEFORE it is executed. `gcloud run jobs execute` has no\n12-# way to select an image ad hoc, so an execute against a stale Job definition silently\n13-# runs last release's migration and reports success (doc 07 §5, and the linked gcloud\n14-# reference). That is the failure this ordering exists to prevent.\n15-\n16-name: deploy staging\n17-\n18-on:\n--\n171-            # Appended per line, not `done | tee file`: the loop's `::error::` annotation is\n172-            # stdout too, and through a trailing tee it became a LINE IN THE ROLLBACK FILE —\n173-            # which the restore step then fed to update-traffic as a service name, so a\n174-            # record-step failure (zero traffic touched) ended in a red \"TRAFFIC RESTORE\n175-            # FAILED\" page. No pre-truncate either: the file exists only once a real line\n176-            # was written, so the hashFiles guards stay inert on an early failure.\n177-            printf '%s %s\\n' \"$svc\" \"$rev\" | tee -a rollback-revisions.txt\n178-          done\n179-\n180-      # Uploaded immediately, not batched with the evidence at the end of the job. The\n181:      # run this file matters most for is the one where the deploy or the smoke fails —\n182-      # a rollback target that only ships on a green run is not a rollback target, and\n183-      # position alone did not buy that: with no `if:` the default is `success()`, so the\n184-      # upload was skipped on exactly the runs it exists for. `overwrite: true` because\n185-      # artifact names are unique per RUN, not per attempt — without it \"Re-run failed\n186-      # jobs\" 409s here and can never reach the deploy step it was re-run to retry.\n187-      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a # v7.0.1\n188-        if: always() && hashFiles('rollback-revisions.txt') != ''\n189-        with:\n190-          name: staging-rollback-revisions\n191-          path: rollback-revisions.txt\n--\n242-          set -euo pipefail\n243-          printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[] | \"\\(.service) \\(.image)\"' |\n244-          while read -r svc image; do\n245-            echo \"::group::$svc\"\n246-            gcloud run services update \"$svc\" \\\n247-              --project \"$PROJECT\" \\\n248-              --region \"$REGION\" \\\n249-              --image \"$image\"\n250-            # A prior staging rollback pins traffic to a revision NAME (rollback.yml's\n251-            # `--to-revisions`), and `services update` does not unpin it: the next deploy\n252:            # creates a 0% revision, the smoke below proves the OLD one, and the evidence\n253-            # records a digest that never served — which production then promotes. A no-op\n254-            # when traffic is already LATEST. Production deliberately does not get this\n255-            # line: it shifts by `--to-tags`.\n256-            gcloud run services update-traffic \"$svc\" \\\n257-              --project \"$PROJECT\" \\\n258-              --region \"$REGION\" \\\n259-              --to-latest\n260-            echo \"::endgroup::\"\n261-          done\n262-\n263:      # doc 04 §4 + the T30 bypass suite. A staging deploy that does not re-prove the\n264:      # origin gate is how the gate silently regresses: nothing else in the pipeline\n265-      # touches it, and the first place you would notice is production.\n266:      - name: origin + Access bypass suite\n267-        env:\n268-          ENVIRONMENT: staging\n269:          # Every other caller of smoke.ts passes a MODE and this one passed none, so\n270:          # smoke.ts saw it undefined and had to guess. `staging` is its own mode: unlike\n271-          # production's pre/post-migration modes there is no tagged revision to address\n272:          # here, and unlike post-deploy this run must also exercise the origin + Access\n273:          # bypass suite rather than only the happy path.\n274-          MODE: staging\n275:          # Empty on repos with no Cloudflare Access app. smoke.ts calls the public\n276:          # hostname through Cloudflare, and an Access-protected hostname answers 302 to\n277-          # the login page without a service token — indistinguishable from a broken\n278-          # deploy. Never echo either value.\n279-          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID }}\n280-          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET }}\n281:        run: bun run deploy/smoke.ts\n282-\n283-      # This step used to build the JSON with `cat > … <<JSON` — an UNQUOTED heredoc\n284-      # delimiter — with ${{ toJSON(inputs.digests) }} and the plan outputs interpolated\n285-      # into the body. That was a working remote-execution path, and the route through it\n286-      # is worth stating exactly, because it defeats the validation step above rather\n287-      # than evading it:\n288-      #\n289-      #   an attacker dispatches digests with a DUPLICATE key, e.g.\n290-      #     {\"svc\":\"$(payload)\",\"svc\":\"sha256:<a real digest>\"}\n291-      #   plan.ts calls JSON.parse, which keeps the LAST value for a duplicate key, so\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
