{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_114","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git show 895849a677cdb2e1a9a48d11b9c73a2ac1a0ae6c:.github/workflows/deploy-production.yml'","aggregated_output":"# T28 reference: production rollout (doc 07 §6, evidence §11).\n#\n# COPY VERBATIM into each app repo as `.github/workflows/deploy-production.yml`.\n#\n# Doc 07 §6 in one line: \"A 'canary' that jumps from 0% to 100% is not a canary.\" The\n# progression below is 0 -> 5 -> 25 -> 100 with an observation hold at each step, and the\n# hold times are workflow inputs because they are policy, not physics.\n#\n# EXPAND migrations only. A contract migration runs in a later release, after the\n# rollback window closes and no compatible old revision remains (doc 07 §6) — this\n# workflow has no path for one and must not grow one.\n\nname: deploy production\n\non:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      # The operator gate receipt doc 07 §11 requires. It is first on the form because it\n      # is first in the job: nothing else runs until it matches. See the `verify the\n      # operator deploy receipt` step for why the gate is a workflow step rather than the\n      # environment protection rule the doc originally named.\n      receipt:\n        description: The production deploy receipt (matches the PROD_DEPLOY_RECEIPT secret)\n        required: true\n        type: string\n      digests:\n        description: 'JSON: {\"<service>\": \"sha256:...\"} — the digests staging proved'\n        required: true\n        type: string\n      # \"the digests staging proved\" was, until now, a description and nothing more —\n      # production accepted any digest that validated against the manifest, so a digest\n      # that never ran anywhere could be promoted straight to production. doc 07 §5-§6\n      # intends production to promote what staging PROVED; this input is what makes that\n      # claim checkable, by naming the run whose evidence has to back it.\n      staging_run_id:\n        description: Run ID of the deploy-staging run that proved these digests\n        required: true\n        type: string\n      hold_seconds:\n        description: Observation hold at each traffic step\n        required: false\n        default: \"300\"\n        type: string\n      skip_migration:\n        description: This release ships no schema change\n        required: false\n        default: false\n        type: boolean\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: deploy-production\n  cancel-in-progress: false\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  rollout:\n    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n    timeout-minutes: 90\n    # doc 07 §11 wants an \"operator gate receipt where the change affects secrets,\n    # production data or migration\", and names the GitHub environment as that receipt.\n    # Naming the environment here is necessary but NOT sufficient: environment protection\n    # rules are plan-gated, and on this account only a PUBLIC repo gets them. Measured\n    # 2026-08-21 — gridwork (public) carries 2 protection rules on `production`; caisson,\n    # gridwork-studio and dev-profile (private) carry ZERO, so on those three there is no\n    # reviewer and no deployment-branch policy and this line buys only secret scoping.\n    # Do not read it as a gate on a repo that has none; check before relying on it.\n    #\n    # The receipt gate below is what actually binds on all four, and this line is still\n    # required for it to work: `PROD_DEPLOY_RECEIPT` is an ENVIRONMENT secret, so without\n    # `environment: production` it resolves to empty and the gate fails closed.\n    environment: production\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write\n      # Reading another run's artifact (the staging evidence, below) needs this. It is the\n      # narrowest scope that lets `download-artifact` cross a run boundary.\n      actions: read\n    steps:\n      # See publish.yml's `select` job for why an unset repository variable is a\n      # vendor-chosen value rather than a failure — an empty `BUN_VERSION_FILE` installs the\n      # newest Bun on a green run instead of erroring.\n      #\n      # This runs ahead of the receipt gate below, which is a deliberate exception to \"the\n      # receipt is first\": it reads no dispatch input, touches no secret, and cannot be\n      # influenced by whoever pressed the button. The receipt is still the first step that\n      # consumes a dispatch INPUT (the ref check below reads only the ref, which picks this\n      # file and cannot be steered by it), and nothing before it can be steered.\n      - name: assert the repository variables are set\n        env:\n          BUN_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n          GCP_PROD_DEPLOYER_SA: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_DEPLOYER_SA }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n          GCP_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n          GCP_WIF_PROVIDER: ${{ vars.GCP_WIF_PROVIDER }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          missing=\"\"\n          for v in BUN_VERSION_FILE GCP_PROD_DEPLOYER_SA GCP_PROD_PROJECT GCP_REGION GCP_REGISTRY GCP_WIF_PROVIDER; do\n            [ -n \"${!v:-}\" ] || missing=\"$missing $v\"\n          done\n          if [ -n \"$missing\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::unset repository variables:$missing\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      # Production dispatches from `main` only. `workflow_dispatch` accepts any ref, and\n      # the ref chooses which copy of THIS FILE and of deploy/*.ts runs under the production\n      # deployer credential `auth` mints below. This step is the fail-fast, not the\n      # boundary: the WIF binding for the production deployer requires refs/heads/main\n      # alongside the environment claim, so from any other ref `auth` below fails on its\n      # own — this step names the reason first instead of leaving an opaque IAM error.\n      # The boundary is the principalSet member on the impersonation binding (a composite\n      # repository_id:environment:ref claim), NOT the provider's attribute_condition — that\n      # gates pool admission by repository and would break the environment-only staging\n      # bindings if it pinned a ref. Reads only the ref, which picks this file and cannot\n      # be steered by it.\n      - name: refuse a dispatch from any ref but main\n        env:\n          REF: ${{ github.ref }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          if [ \"$REF\" != \"refs/heads/main\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::production deploys dispatch from refs/heads/main only, not ${REF}\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n\n      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n        with:\n          bun-version-file: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n\n      # The operator gate receipt doc 07 §11 requires, and the FIRST thing this job does\n      # after it can run a script at all. Doc 07 names the `production` environment's\n      # required reviewers as that receipt; measured 2026-08-21, environment protection is\n      # plan-gated and only the one PUBLIC repo of the four has any, so on three of them\n      # every `workflow_dispatch` reached production unreviewed. The gate is therefore a\n      # step: the dispatcher supplies `receipt`, the workflow holds `PROD_DEPLOY_RECEIPT`\n      # as an environment secret, and a mismatch ends the run before `auth` mints a\n      # production credential.\n      #\n      # It runs before the dependency install (and before every other check) on purpose,\n      # which is why `verify-receipt.ts` is the one deploy script with no imports outside\n      # `node:`. The comparison is SHA-256-prehash + `timingSafeEqual` per\n      # identity/security.md — see that file's header for why a raw compare on two\n      # variable-length operator strings is a length oracle rather than a comparison.\n      #\n      # What this is NOT: proof a second person reviewed the change. It proves the\n      # dispatcher held a secret. A possession check, not a four-eyes gate.\n      - name: verify the operator deploy receipt\n        env:\n          RECEIPT: ${{ inputs.receipt }}\n          PROD_DEPLOY_RECEIPT: ${{ secrets.PROD_DEPLOY_RECEIPT }}\n        run: bun run deploy/verify-receipt.ts\n\n      # See publish.yml for why this is not an unconditional `bun install` and what the\n      # `exit 1` arm catches. The bodies must not drift.\n      - name: install the deploy scripts' dependencies\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          if [ -f bun.lock ]; then\n            bun install --frozen-lockfile\n          elif grep -hoE 'from \"[^\"]+\"' deploy/*.ts | grep -qvE 'from \"(\\.|node:|bun:)'; then\n            echo \"::error::deploy/*.ts imports a package but this repo has no root bun.lock\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      # `hold_seconds` is free text on a dispatch form and ends up at `sleep \"$HOLD\"`. It\n      # is quoted there, so it is not an injection — the damage is different. An\n      # unparseable value fails `sleep` halfway through a live rollout, with traffic\n      # already split. And a value the form will happily accept — 0, or 86400 — silently\n      # defeats the observation gate that is the entire reason the progression has steps\n      # at all. Checked here, before any credential or cloud mutation, where a bad value\n      # costs nothing but a red run.\n      #\n      # The `?????*` arm (five or more characters) is not cosmetic and it is not a length\n      # policy. `[ \"$HOLD\" -lt 30 ]` on a 20-digit value exits 2 with \"integer expected\",\n      # and because that test sits in an `if` condition `set -e` does not fire and a\n      # status of 2 reads as FALSE — so both range tests errored, the `if` was false, and\n      # the validator PASSED the exact class of value it exists to stop. Capping the\n      # digits first keeps the arithmetic inside what `[` can evaluate.\n      #\n      # The ceiling is 900, not 3600, and that is a correctness bound rather than taste:\n      # the progression holds three times, so an accepted 3600 guarantees 10800s of sleep\n      # against this job's `timeout-minutes: 90`. The job is then CANCELLED mid-rollout\n      # with traffic parked at 5% or 25%. 3 x 900 = 45 minutes leaves the other half of\n      # the budget for checkout, plan, migration and the three smokes. Raising it means\n      # raising `timeout-minutes` in the same edit; they are one number in two places.\n      #\n      # Neither message echoes the submitted value back. A dispatch input can carry a\n      # newline, and a newline in an `::error::` line lets the rest of the value start a\n      # workflow command of its own (`::stop-commands::` suppresses every later\n      # annotation, including the traffic-restore alarm). The operator knows what they\n      # typed; the message says what is required instead.\n      - name: validate the rollout inputs\n        env:\n          HOLD: ${{ inputs.hold_seconds }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          case \"$HOLD\" in\n            ''|*[!0-9]*|?????*)\n              echo \"::error::hold_seconds must be a whole number of seconds, at most 4 digits\"\n              exit 1\n              ;;\n          esac\n          if [ \"$HOLD\" -lt 30 ] || [ \"$HOLD\" -gt 900 ]; then\n            echo \"::error::hold_seconds must be between 30 and 900 inclusive (three holds must fit inside timeout-minutes)\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - name: validate inputs against the manifest\n        id: plan\n        env:\n          DIGESTS: ${{ inputs.digests }}\n          ENVIRONMENT: production\n          # See deploy-staging.yml — plan.ts cannot build an image reference without it.\n          GCP_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n        run: bun run deploy/plan.ts >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n      # plan.ts proves the digests are well-formed and named in the manifest. It cannot\n      # prove they were ever run. Both checks sit BEFORE `auth` on purpose: a request with\n      # no staging provenance behind it should never reach a production credential.\n      - name: fetch the staging evidence this promotion claims\n        uses: actions/download-artifact@3e5f45b2cfb9172054b4087a40e8e0b5a5461e7c # v8.0.1\n        with:\n          name: staging-evidence\n          path: staging-evidence\n          run-id: ${{ inputs.staging_run_id }}\n          github-token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n\n      - name: assert every digest was proved in staging\n        env:\n          DIGESTS: ${{ inputs.digests }}\n          STAGING_RUN_ID: ${{ inputs.staging_run_id }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          # deploy-staging.yml records its digests through a toJSON() of the dispatch\n          # input, which renders the JSON *string* that run was dispatched with, not an\n          # object. Accept either shape rather than let this gate hinge on that detail\n          # staying put. (Written without an expression even in this comment: the runner\n          # substitutes over the whole script text, comments included.)\n          # `download-artifact` with a dispatcher-chosen `run-id` will return an artifact\n          # named `staging-evidence` from ANY run in this repo — nothing about the fetch\n          # says the run was a staging deploy. Assert it from the evidence itself. This is\n          # not a privilege boundary and is not claimed as one: dispatching production and\n          # uploading an artifact both require repo write, so it adds no escalation\n          # surface. It is an operator-error gate, and it is the difference between \"some\n          # run produced a file with this name\" and \"a staging deploy produced it\".\n          env_claimed=\"$(jq -r '.environment // \"\"' staging-evidence/staging-evidence.json)\"\n          if [ \"$env_claimed\" != \"staging\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::the named run's evidence is not a staging deploy\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n          staged=\"$(jq -c '.digests | if type == \"string\" then fromjson else . end' \\\n            staging-evidence/staging-evidence.json)\"\n          # SUBSET, not equality: promoting one service out of a two-service staging run is\n          # a legitimate release, so a staging run covering MORE than this request is fine.\n          # What is not fine is a (service, digest) pair that staging never saw, or saw at\n          # a different digest. Read DIGESTS out of the environment inside jq — it never\n          # becomes a shell argument.\n          # gsub before join: the keys come out of the dispatcher's own JSON, and a JSON\n          # key may contain a newline. Echoed into an `::error::` line below, a newline\n          # lets the remainder of the key start a workflow command of its own — a key\n          # ending in a line reading `::stop-commands::<token>` suppresses every\n          # annotation after it, including the traffic-restore alarm this rollout depends\n          # on being visible. Flatten the value where it is produced, once.\n          unproved=\"$(jq -rcn --argjson staged \"$staged\" \\\n            '(env.DIGESTS | fromjson)\n             | to_entries\n             | map(select($staged[.key] != .value) | .key | gsub(\"[\\\\r\\\\n]\"; \" \"))\n             | join(\", \")')\"\n          if [ -n \"$unproved\" ]; then\n            # Name every one of them. A gate that reports only the first failure gets run\n            # N times to discover N problems, and each of those runs is a dispatch.\n            echo \"::error::not proved by staging run ${STAGING_RUN_ID}: ${unproved}\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - uses: google-github-actions/auth@7c6bc770dae815cd3e89ee6cdf493a5fab2cc093 # v3.0.0\n        with:\n          workload_identity_provider: ${{ vars.GCP_WIF_PROVIDER }}\n          service_account: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_DEPLOYER_SA }}\n          create_credentials_file: true\n\n      - uses: google-github-actions/setup-gcloud@aa5489c8933f4cc7a4f7d45035b3b1440c9c10db # v3.0.1\n\n      # R44 (2026-08-25): the verification point for publish.yml's keyless signature. The\n      # staging-evidence gate above proves these digests RAN; this proves they were BUILT\n      # by this repository's publish workflow on refs/heads/main. It is an identity check\n      # against the Fulcio certificate — the workflow path and ref that GitHub's OIDC\n      # token carried when the digest was signed — not a key. A digest pushed by hand with\n      # the publisher credential, or built by a dispatch from another ref, fails here,\n      # before the rollback revision is even recorded and long before a revision exists.\n      #\n      # It sits AFTER `auth` because the registry is private: cosign has to fetch the\n      # signature next to the image, so it logs in with the deployer's own access token.\n      # The login host is GCP_REGISTRY's first segment. publish.yml asserts that\n      # GCP_REGISTRY_HOST is exactly that segment before it sends the PUBLISHER's token\n      # anywhere; deriving the host here instead of reading a second variable means no\n      # variable can point this token at a host the image does not live on.\n      #\n      # Every image the plan produced, not just the first: `while read` over a resolved\n      # variable, asserted non-empty, for the reasons the URL step below spells out. A\n      # failing `cosign verify` aborts under `set -e` and names the image in its own error.\n      - uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@6f9f17788090df1f26f669e9d70d6ae9567deba6 # v4.1.2\n        with:\n          cosign-release: v3.1.3\n\n      - name: verify every digest was signed by publish on main\n        env:\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}\n          GCP_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          images=\"$(printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[].image')\"\n          if [ -z \"$images\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::the plan produced no images — refusing to deploy what was never verified\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n          gcloud auth print-access-token |\n            cosign login \"${GCP_REGISTRY%%/*}\" --username oauth2accesstoken --password-stdin\n          identity=\"https://github.com/${REPOSITORY}/.github/workflows/publish-image.yml@refs/heads/main\"\n          while read -r image; do\n            cosign verify \\\n              --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \\\n              --certificate-identity \"$identity\" \\\n              \"$image\"\n          done <<< \"$images\"\n\n      # Recorded BEFORE anything changes. doc 07 §11 wants the rollback revision in the\n      # evidence, and the only moment it is knowable is before the new revision exists.\n      - name: record the rollback revision\n        id: previous\n        # Every value below arrives through the environment, not through `${{ }}` in the\n        # script. The runner substitutes `${{ }}` into the script SOURCE before any shell\n        # sees it, so a value containing `$(...)` lands as executable text; single-quoting\n        # it is not a fix, because a value containing a quote closes the quoting. An\n        # `env:` mapping puts the value in the process environment instead, where it is\n        # data. This holds for every `run:` block in this file.\n        env:\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          # See deploy-staging.yml's record step: `status.traffic[0]` was an array position\n          # held up by gcloud's undocumented serialisation order. Select by percent; refuse\n          # on a split (rollback.yml first, then deploy).\n          serving='[.status.traffic[]? | select((.percent // 0) > 0) | .revisionName]\n            | if length == 1 then .[0] else error(\"expected exactly one serving revision, got \\(length)\") end'\n          printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[].service' |\n          while read -r svc; do\n            # Fail loudly on any error EXCEPT \"the service does not exist\", which records\n            # nothing. Bare suppression (`|| true`) would turn an expired credential or a\n            # missing run.viewer binding into a green run with no rollback target. NOT_FOUND\n            # is NOT the first-deploy path: every service is pre-created by the production\n            # stack with a serving bootstrap revision (modules/cloud_run_service — image and\n            # traffic under ignore_changes), so a first CI deploy always records one, and\n            # `services update` below cannot create a service — an absent one fails there\n            # at zero traffic, before any tag or shift exists.\n            if ! rev=\"$(gcloud run services describe \"$svc\" \\\n              --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n              --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n              --format json 2>describe-err.txt | jq -r \"$serving\")\"; then\n              if ! grep -qi 'NOT_FOUND\\|not found' describe-err.txt; then\n                cat describe-err.txt >&2\n                echo \"::error::could not read the serving revision of ${svc} — refusing to roll out without a rollback target\"\n                exit 1\n              fi\n              rev=\"\"\n            fi\n            # No line rather than a line naming nothing. \"svc \" is an exact whole-line\n            # match for rollback.yml's `grep -Fxq \"$SERVICE $REVISION\"` when the operator\n            # submits an empty revision, which would let the provenance gate approve a\n            # revision that never held traffic.\n            [ -n \"$rev\" ] || continue\n            # Appended per line, not `done | tee file`: the loop's `::error::` annotation is\n            # stdout too, and through a trailing tee it became a LINE IN THE ROLLBACK FILE —\n            # which the restore step then fed to update-traffic as a service name, so a\n            # record-step failure (zero traffic touched) ended in a red \"TRAFFIC RESTORE\n            # FAILED\" page. No pre-truncate either: the file exists only once a real line\n            # was written, so the hashFiles guards stay inert on an early failure.\n            printf '%s %s\\n' \"$svc\" \"$rev\" | tee -a rollback-revisions.txt\n          done\n\n      # §6 step 1: zero traffic, tagged. The revision exists and is addressable by tag,\n      # and no user has reached it.\n      - name: deploy zero-traffic tagged revisions\n        env:\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          tag=\"r${GITHUB_RUN_ID}\"\n          echo \"CANARY_TAG=$tag\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[] | \"\\(.service) \\(.image)\"' |\n          while read -r svc image; do\n            gcloud run services update \"$svc\" \\\n              --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n              --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n              --image \"$image\" \\\n              --tag \"$tag\" \\\n              --no-traffic\n          done\n\n      # doc 07 §6 step 5 asks for the tagged revision to be smoke-tested BEFORE any\n      # traffic reaches it, and the first cut of this template had no way to do it. A\n      # Cloud Run traffic tag is addressable only at its own run.app URL; the T27 origin\n      # gate rejects a direct run.app request by design; and Cloudflare routes to the\n      # service's default URL, never to a tag. The caisson and gridwork adoptions hit\n      # this independently and both degraded the pre-traffic smokes to public-edge\n      # baselines against the CURRENT revision — which is not a canary check at all, and\n      # would have left the 5% step as the canary's first real exercise.\n      #\n      # Operator ruling 2026-08-21: the deploy workflow carries the origin secret and\n      # calls the tag URL directly, satisfying the gate as the caller rather than routing\n      # around it. Resolve the URLs from the service description — do not synthesize them\n      # from the tag name, because a URL that is merely well-formed proves nothing.\n      - name: resolve the tagged revision URLs\n        env:\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          # The service list is resolved into a variable FIRST, and asserted non-empty,\n          # rather than being read from `< <(printf … | jq …)`. A process substitution has\n          # no exit-status path at all: if jq rejects $SERVICES the loop runs ZERO times,\n          # every guard below it is skipped, and the step exits 0 having proved nothing —\n          # `CANARY_URLS={}` then reaches smoke.ts, which has a public-hostname path and\n          # passes against the CURRENT revision. A failing command substitution in an\n          # assignment does abort under `set -e`, and `pipefail` carries the jq failure\n          # out of the pipe, so this shape fails where the other silently succeeded.\n          svcs=\"$(printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[].service')\"\n          if [ -z \"$svcs\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::the plan produced no services — refusing to report a canary that was never resolved\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n          urls='{}'\n          while read -r svc; do\n            # `first(...)` rather than a bare filter: two traffic entries carrying the same\n            # tag would otherwise make $url multiline, and every check below assumes one\n            # line. `// \"\"` turns \"no match\" into the empty string the next guard expects.\n            url=\"$(gcloud run services describe \"$svc\" \\\n              --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n              --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n              --format json |\n              jq -rc --arg t \"$CANARY_TAG\" 'first(.status.traffic[] | select(.tag == $t) | .url) // \"\" | gsub(\"[\\\\r\\\\n]\"; \"\")')\"\n            # Fail loudly. An empty URL here would otherwise reach smoke.ts, which has a\n            # public-hostname path and would happily pass against the CURRENT revision —\n            # a green pre-traffic check that tested nothing.\n            if [ -z \"$url\" ]; then\n              echo \"::error::no URL for tag ${CANARY_TAG} on ${svc}\"\n              exit 1\n            fi\n            # This URL is the address the next two steps send $ORIGIN_SECRETS to. Whatever\n            # it names receives the production origin secret, so it has to be a Cloud Run\n            # host and nothing else — the check is on the value that will actually be\n            # called, not on the shape of the tag it was derived from.\n            #\n            # `[[ =~ ]]` and not `grep -Eq`: grep anchors per LINE, so a two-line value\n            # whose first line is a run.app host passes grep while carrying a second host\n            # along with it. Bash anchors against the whole string, which is what \"this is\n            # the only host we will talk to\" actually means. The gsub above additionally\n            # strips newlines at the source, so the value echoed in the refusal below\n            # cannot start a workflow command of its own.\n            run_app_re='^https://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.run\\.app$'\n            if [[ ! \"$url\" =~ $run_app_re ]]; then\n              echo \"::error::${svc}: resolved tag URL is not a Cloud Run host, refusing to send the origin secret to it: ${url}\"\n              exit 1\n            fi\n            # -c on every jq that feeds a shell variable. Without it jq pretty-prints, and\n            # this accumulator is what gets written to $GITHUB_ENV below as one line.\n            urls=\"$(printf '%s' \"$urls\" | jq -c --arg k \"$svc\" --arg v \"$url\" '. + {($k): $v}')\"\n          done <<< \"$svcs\"\n          # One line, no heredoc. The heredoc this replaced used a FIXED delimiter, so a\n          # value containing that delimiter on a line of its own closed the block early and\n          # everything after it became attacker-chosen environment variables for every\n          # later step — the documented Actions env-injection class. A single-line write\n          # cannot be extended: a newline in the value would break the write, not smuggle\n          # variables past it, and jq -c above guarantees there is none.\n          printf 'CANARY_URLS=%s\\n' \"$urls\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      # §6 step 2: checks that do NOT require the new schema. Running schema-dependent\n      # checks here would fail every expand release for the wrong reason and train\n      # everyone to ignore this gate.\n      #\n      # CANARY_TAG and CANARY_URLS are inherited from $GITHUB_ENV and deliberately NOT\n      # re-declared here: a step-level `env:` mapping overrides the inherited value, so a\n      # passthrough that expanded to empty would blank them and take smoke.ts down its\n      # public-hostname path without saying so.\n      - name: pre-migration boot + origin checks\n        env:\n          ENVIRONMENT: production\n          MODE: pre-migration\n          # The per-service origin secrets, as a JSON object keyed by service key, so one\n          # environment secret covers a multi-service repo and this file stays identical\n          # across all four. smoke.ts sends its repo's own origin header — the header NAME\n          # is per-repo and belongs in that repo's smoke.ts, only the value comes from here.\n          ORIGIN_SECRETS: ${{ secrets.ORIGIN_SECRETS }}\n          # Empty on repos with no Cloudflare Access app. smoke.ts calls the public\n          # hostname through Cloudflare, and an Access-protected hostname answers 302 to\n          # the login page without a service token — indistinguishable from a broken\n          # deploy. Never echo either value.\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID }}\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET }}\n        run: bun run deploy/smoke.ts\n\n      # §6 steps 3-4. Update-then-execute, same as staging, same reason. Fail closed:\n      # a partially applied expand migration with traffic still on the old revision is\n      # recoverable; the same migration with traffic already shifted is not.\n      # The `if:` keys keep their `${{ }}` — expression keys are evaluated, never pasted\n      # into a shell. Only the script bodies had to move to `env:`.\n      - name: point the migration Job at this digest\n        if: inputs.skip_migration != true && steps.plan.outputs.migration_job != ''\n        env:\n          MIGRATION_JOB: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.migration_job }}\n          MIGRATION_IMAGE: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.migration_image }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          gcloud run jobs update \"$MIGRATION_JOB\" \\\n            --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n            --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n            --image \"$MIGRATION_IMAGE\"\n\n      - name: execute the expand migration\n        if: inputs.skip_migration != true && steps.plan.outputs.migration_job != ''\n        env:\n          MIGRATION_JOB: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.migration_job }}\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          gcloud run jobs execute \"$MIGRATION_JOB\" \\\n            --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n            --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n            --wait\n\n      # §6 step 5: authenticated origin headers, representative read AND write flows.\n      # Read-only smoke on a release that changed writes proves nothing.\n      - name: smoke the tagged revision\n        env:\n          ENVIRONMENT: production\n          MODE: post-migration\n          # As above: CANARY_TAG / CANARY_URLS are inherited, never re-declared.\n          ORIGIN_SECRETS: ${{ secrets.ORIGIN_SECRETS }}\n          # Empty on repos with no Cloudflare Access app. smoke.ts calls the public\n          # hostname through Cloudflare, and an Access-protected hostname answers 302 to\n          # the login page without a service token — indistinguishable from a broken\n          # deploy. Never echo either value.\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID }}\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET }}\n        run: bun run deploy/smoke.ts\n\n      # §6 steps 6-8. Each step observes before the next shift; deploy/observe.ts is the\n      # gate and it must exit non-zero on a breach, not warn. An observation step that\n      # cannot fail is decoration.\n      - name: progressive traffic shift\n        id: shift\n        env:\n          HOLD: ${{ inputs.hold_seconds }}\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          ENVIRONMENT: production\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          # Resolved into a variable and asserted non-empty for the same reason as the URL\n          # step: `done < <(… | jq …)` swallows a jq failure entirely, and a step that\n          # shifts traffic to zero services while exiting 0 is a fully green rollout that\n          # deployed nothing (observe.ts passes, because the OLD revision is healthy).\n          svcs=\"$(printf '%s' \"$SERVICES\" | jq -r '.[].service')\"\n          if [ -z \"$svcs\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::the plan produced no services — nothing to shift traffic to\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n          for pct in 5 25 100; do\n            echo \"::group::shift to ${pct}%\"\n            while read -r svc; do\n              gcloud run services update-traffic \"$svc\" \\\n                --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n                --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n                --to-tags \"${CANARY_TAG}=${pct}\"\n            done <<< \"$svcs\"\n            sleep \"$HOLD\"\n            # ENVIRONMENT / GCP_PROD_PROJECT / GCP_REGION are inherited from the step env\n            # above; only STEP varies per iteration.\n            STEP=\"$pct\" bun run deploy/observe.ts\n            echo \"::endgroup::\"\n          done\n\n      # §6 step 9. The previous revision is retained deliberately — `update-traffic` does\n      # not delete it, and nothing here should. It is the rollback target.\n      - name: post-deploy synthetic flows\n        env:\n          ENVIRONMENT: production\n          MODE: post-deploy\n          # Traffic is at 100% and the promoted revision is what the public hostname\n          # serves, so this one goes through Cloudflare — the only step that proves the\n          # real end-to-end path. Blanked here rather than left to smoke.ts's discipline:\n          # the tag URL is still valid at this point, so a mode bug would otherwise smoke\n          # the canary a third time and never test the edge at all.\n          CANARY_URLS: \"\"\n          ORIGIN_SECRETS: \"\"\n          # Empty on repos with no Cloudflare Access app. smoke.ts calls the public\n          # hostname through Cloudflare, and an Access-protected hostname answers 302 to\n          # the login page without a service token — indistinguishable from a broken\n          # deploy. Never echo either value.\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID }}\n          CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET: ${{ secrets.CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET }}\n        run: bun run deploy/smoke.ts\n\n      # Put traffic back on the revisions recorded before this run started.\n      #\n      # This is a TRAFFIC restore, NOT a database rollback. doc 07 §8 is explicit that the\n      # two are not the same thing: the expand migration executed above is not reversed\n      # here and never will be. That makes this safe only while the old revision still\n      # reads what the new one wrote — which is exactly the window an expand migration is\n      # designed to hold open. Past that window the recorded revision is not a safe target\n      # and the approved database recovery procedure is the right tool, not this.\n      #\n      # A STEP, not an `ERR` trap inside the shift step, and the difference is three\n      # separate defects the trap form had:\n      #\n      #   1. `set -eEuo` propagates the trap into a process substitution, so a jq failure\n      #      in `done < <(…)` ran the handler INSIDE that subshell: it printed its own\n      #      `::error::` onto the very fd `while read` was consuming, issued a real\n      #      update-traffic for every service, exited the subshell only — and the outer\n      #      loop then fed the handler's own message to gcloud as a service name while the\n      #      step exited 0. Reproduced.\n      #   2. Each `run:` is its own process, so the trap could not see a failure in\n      #      `post-deploy synthetic flows` — the only step that proves the real Cloudflare\n      #      path. Traffic was already at 100% by then, with no restore.\n      #   3. A cancelled job (the operator pressing Cancel, or `timeout-minutes` expiring\n      #      mid-hold) delivers a signal. An `ERR` trap does not fire on a signal, so the\n      #      case most likely to strand traffic was the one case never covered.\n      #\n      # `failure() || cancelled()` covers all three, and the hashFiles guard makes it an\n      # inert no-op on a run that failed before the record step. The alarm is deliberately\n      # separate from the cause: a restore that itself failed means production is still\n      # serving the canary and needs a human now, and it must not read as the thing that\n      # broke.\n      - name: restore traffic if the rollout did not complete\n        id: restore\n        if: (failure() || cancelled()) && hashFiles('rollback-revisions.txt') != ''\n        env:\n          GCP_PROD_PROJECT: ${{ vars.GCP_PROD_PROJECT }}\n          GCP_REGION: ${{ vars.GCP_REGION }}\n        run: |\n          set -uo pipefail\n          echo \"::error::rollout did not complete — restoring traffic to the revisions recorded before this run\"\n          failed=0\n          while read -r svc rev; do\n            # The producer above omits lines with no revision, so reaching one here means\n            # the file was written by something else. Say so and count it: skipping in\n            # silence let the restore report clean while a service still served the canary.\n            if [ -z \"${rev:-}\" ]; then\n              echo \"::error::no recorded revision for ${svc} — restore it by hand\"\n              failed=1\n              continue\n            fi\n            gcloud run services update-traffic \"$svc\" \\\n              --project \"$GCP_PROD_PROJECT\" \\\n              --region \"$GCP_REGION\" \\\n              --to-revisions \"${rev}=100\" || failed=1\n          done < rollback-revisions.txt\n          # `|| echo` alone exits 0 — this step read GREEN while its own annotation said\n          # production may still be serving the canary, and the reader triaging the red job\n          # was sent to the wrong step. `-e` stays off above so the loop finishes every\n          # service before reporting; the exit is here instead.\n          [ \"$failed\" -eq 0 ] || {\n            echo \"::error::TRAFFIC RESTORE FAILED — production may still be serving the canary; restore by hand from rollback-revisions.txt\"\n            exit 1\n          }\n\n      # No `if:` meant the default `success()`: the evidence was skipped on exactly the runs\n      # it exists for — a failed shift, a failed smoke, a restore — and the run that\n      # stranded traffic left no record of where it stopped. Same shape rollback.yml\n      # already uses: `always()`, narrowed to runs where the plan and the record step\n      # produced the inputs this needs, with the shift's and the restore's own outcomes\n      # inside the file. `skipped` for the restore is the normal value on a clean run.\n      #\n      # `workflow_commit`, not `commit` — see deploy-staging.yml's evidence step.\n      - name: deployment evidence\n        if: always() && steps.plan.outcome == 'success' && hashFiles('rollback-revisions.txt') != ''\n        env:\n          COMMIT: ${{ github.sha }}\n          RUN_ID: ${{ github.run_id }}\n          STAGING_RUN_ID: ${{ inputs.staging_run_id }}\n          SERVICES: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.services }}\n          MIGRATION: ${{ inputs.skip_migration && 'skipped' || steps.plan.outputs.migration_job }}\n          ROLLOUT: ${{ steps.shift.outcome }}\n          RESTORE: ${{ steps.restore.outcome }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          # jq -n was already the right call, but the values were still reaching it as\n          # SHELL ARGUMENTS pasted in by the runner — `--argjson services` took a\n          # single-quoted expression, which ends its own quoting at the first single quote\n          # in the value, and the remainder is command source. Through `env:` each value is\n          # one argv element, quoted by the shell, and jq is the only thing that parses it.\n          #\n          # Captured, then written — the pattern rollback.yml's evidence step wrote down\n          # (T28-AUDIT-3). A direct `jq -n … > production-evidence.json` opens the\n          # redirect before jq runs, so a jq refusal — a malformed $SERVICES from an\n          # adopter's plan is the one failable input — left a ZERO-BYTE file, and the\n          # upload below is guarded by hashFiles over this file OR rollback-revisions.txt,\n          # which this step's own `if:` guarantees exists: the guard was satisfied\n          # regardless and the empty file published as the deploy's evidence of record.\n          # Under `set -euo pipefail` a failed jq aborts the assignment before any file\n          # exists, and the upload carries the revisions file alone — a red step with\n          # honest evidence instead of a green artifact with none.\n          evidence=\"$(jq -n \\\n            --arg workflow_commit \"$COMMIT\" \\\n            --arg run_id \"$RUN_ID\" \\\n            --arg staging_run_id \"$STAGING_RUN_ID\" \\\n            --arg tag \"${CANARY_TAG:-}\" \\\n            --argjson services \"$SERVICES\" \\\n            --arg migration \"$MIGRATION\" \\\n            --arg rollout \"$ROLLOUT\" \\\n            --arg restore \"$RESTORE\" \\\n            --rawfile rollback rollback-revisions.txt \\\n            '{environment:\"production\", workflow_commit:$workflow_commit, run_id:$run_id,\n              staging_run_id:$staging_run_id, canary_tag:$tag,\n              services:$services, migration:$migration, rollback_revisions:$rollback,\n              rollout:$rollout, restore:$restore,\n              traffic_progression:[0,5,25,100]}')\"\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$evidence\" > production-evidence.json\n\n      # `if: always()` with `if-no-files-found: error` was a guaranteed second red step on\n      # any run that failed in input validation, plan, or the provenance gate: neither\n      # path exists yet at that point, so the upload errored on top of the real failure.\n      # The hashFiles guard is the same one the dedicated upload below already carried.\n      # `overwrite: true` because artifact names are unique per RUN, not per attempt —\n      # without it \"Re-run failed jobs\" 409s on the second attempt.\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a # v7.0.1\n        if: always() && hashFiles('production-evidence.json', 'rollback-revisions.txt') != ''\n        with:\n          name: production-evidence\n          path: |\n            production-evidence.json\n            rollback-revisions.txt\n          if-no-files-found: error\n          overwrite: true\n\n      # The same file again, under its own name. rollback.yml's provenance check fetches\n      # the recorded revisions by artifact name, and `production-evidence` is a bundle\n      # whose file list is expected to grow — a dedicated name is a stable contract for\n      # that fetch, where \"some file inside a bundle\" is not. The hashFiles guard keeps a\n      # run that failed before the record step from adding a second red step to a run that\n      # already has one; `if-no-files-found: error` still applies once the file exists.\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@043fb46d1a93c77aae656e7c1c64a875d1fc6a0a # v7.0.1\n        if: always() && hashFiles('rollback-revisions.txt') != ''\n        with:\n          name: production-rollback-revisions\n          path: rollback-revisions.txt\n          if-no-files-found: error\n          overwrite: true\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
